<div class="ai-research-essay" data-text-revision="be563816c359">
<p class="standfirst">If you are moving deeper into abstraction to outrun AI, you are too late. OpenAI supplied the missing upper bound in the Deligne-Drinfeld conjecture. In characteristic two, its proof set a variable to zero and showed that the defining relations still determined the whole polynomial. The machine decided what the proof could afford to throw away.</p><p class="standfirst">A diagram can expose the relation a proof must establish or a calculation must preserve. That relation can suggest an ansatz to try and tell us what the result has to guarantee. Where would I first put a mathematician and an agent up against two agents? In the incidence geometry of numerical PDE, while the old grid still exists and there is still time to decide what to measure. A changing mesh lets us exhibit which answer the new record loses, then ask what it would cost to save enough information to recover that answer at the scientist’s tolerance.</p><section id="a-rapidly-changing-advantage"><h2>1. What a correct calculation can erase</h2><p>Take a ring of unit circumference with four equal cells. Dye the odd cells in one run and the even cells in another. The concentration is constant within each old cell, one in the dyed cells and zero in the others. Shift the grid by half a cell. Four averages go in and four come out. The four outputs are all one half in both runs. The total is exactly right. How much dye was in cell one? The answer is one quarter in one run and zero in the other. Any answer computed from the new averages alone misses by at least one eighth in one of the two runs. <a class="citation" id="cite-147-1" href="#ref-147" aria-label="Source 147">[147]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-151-1" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a></p><figure class="figure"><div role="img" aria-label="Old concentrations 1, 0, 1, 0 and 0, 1, 0, 1 both transfer to four new averages of one half. The old alternating sums are plus two and minus two, respectively." style="display:grid;align-items:center;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr) auto minmax(0,1fr);gap:.4rem .55rem;font:.85rem/1.4 var(--sans)"><strong>Old cell concentrations</strong><span></span><strong>New cell averages</strong><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.15rem"><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--refactor-mint) 26%,var(--surface))">1</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--surface)">0</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--refactor-mint) 26%,var(--surface))">1</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--surface)">0</span></div><span aria-hidden="true" style="color:var(--refactor-mint)">→</span><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.15rem"><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--wash)">½</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--wash)">½</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--wash)">½</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--wash)">½</span></div><div style="grid-column:1 / -1;margin-bottom:.45rem;color:var(--muted)">Alternating sum <strong style="color:var(--ink)">+2</strong></div><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.15rem"><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--surface)">0</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--refactor-mint) 26%,var(--surface))">1</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--surface)">0</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:color-mix(in srgb,var(--refactor-mint) 26%,var(--surface))">1</span></div><span aria-hidden="true" style="color:var(--refactor-mint)">→</span><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.15rem"><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--wash)">½</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--wash)">½</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--wash)">½</span><span style="padding:.4rem .08rem;text-align:center;border:1px solid var(--frame);background:var(--wash)">½</span></div><div style="grid-column:1 / -1;margin-bottom:.45rem;color:var(--muted)">Alternating sum <strong style="color:var(--ink)">−2</strong></div></div><figcaption>Both runs produce the same new cell averages. The alternating sum of the old concentrations, odd cells minus even cells, distinguishes them.</figcaption></figure><p>Ask for the total in two adjacent old cells and the same record gives it exactly. One new average measures those two old concentrations with equal weight. Save the odd-cell sum minus the even-cell sum and every old cell average is recoverable. Agents chose this example and found the extra measurement after I gave them numerical PDE as the field. <a class="citation" id="cite-151-2" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a></p><p>A record determines the exact answer to a question if every admissible input that produces the record gives the same answer. Two fields that agree on the record and differ on the answer prove that the record is insufficient. Once those fields have become the same record, no advance in solver power can separate them from that record alone. The time to change what the next investigator can find out is while the old field is still available.</p><p>A failed test gives both teams a reason to investigate. A passing test leaves them a harder question. The grid transfer kept its promise to conserve the dye. A scientist who needs the mass on a patch has reason to demand a different promise. The brief can name the family of possible patches, the error tolerance and the storage budget before the scientist knows which patch she will need. On fresh meshes, give both teams the same total research budget. See which more often spots a loss the passing check conceals, decides <a href="#testing-the-proposed-advantage">what to save</a> while the old field is still available, and proves that the saved record recovers every required patch mass within tolerance. <a class="citation" id="cite-101-1" href="#ref-101" aria-label="Source 101">[101]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-105-1" href="#ref-105" aria-label="Source 105">[105]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-130-1" href="#ref-130" aria-label="Source 130">[130]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-147-2" href="#ref-147" aria-label="Source 147">[147]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-151-3" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a></p></section><section id="beyond-verbal-imitation"><h2>2. What the OpenAI breakthroughs actually show</h2><p>A proof can turn on deciding what to forget. The Deligne-Drinfeld conjecture identifies the rational Grothendieck-Teichmüller Lie algebra with the free graded Lie algebra on one generator in each odd degree starting at three. A rational lower bound was known. Thomas Willwacher credits OpenAI with the missing upper bound and gives a shorter, AI-assisted proof. In each weight the defining equations form a linear system with integer coefficients. Reducing its matrix modulo two can lower the rank, so an upper bound on the nullity there also bounds the rational dimension. <a class="citation" id="cite-1-1" href="#ref-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></p><p>In characteristic two write A = x², B = y and C = [x,y]. Now set A to zero. At weight three the projection does this. <a class="citation" id="cite-1-2" href="#ref-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="P₃ = [A,B] + [B,C],    P₃(0,B,C) = [B,C].">P₃ = [A,B] + [B,C],    P₃(0,B,C) = [B,C].</div><p>For an arbitrary Lie polynomial, setting A to zero erases any multiple of [A,B]. What saves the proof is the restriction to polynomials satisfying the defining equations. A representation of the four-strand braid Lie algebra turns those equations into a formula recovering the whole polynomial from its projection. On that solution space the projection is injective. Its image lies in Lie(C,[B,C]), small enough to count in weights at least three. The upper bound was the missing half of the conjecture. OpenAI also gave an independent argument for injectivity of the original map from the free graded Lie algebra, for which Brown’s earlier work already supplied the rational lower bound. <a class="citation" id="cite-1-3" href="#ref-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></p><p>A correct proof left another piece of mathematics to invent. Leila Schneps and Benjamin Enriquez reworked the argument. Willwacher credits them with inspiring a simpler formula for the four-strand representation, and credits AI tools with helping him understand and restructure the proof. Nikita Markarian identified the projection with the leading part of a geometric operation. Take the double cover that sends z to z² and fill a puncture. He constructed an inverse on the image and recovered the algebraic reconstruction in leading terms. That interpretation is a new result to use. It does not retroactively give the interpreters the original upper bound. <a class="citation" id="cite-1-4" href="#ref-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-2-1" href="#ref-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></p><p>OpenAI’s model used number fields of growing degree to put unexpectedly many pairs of points a unit distance apart in the plane. Nine mathematicians verified, simplified and generalized the unit-distance construction. Bloom, Sawin, Schildkraut and Zhelezov saw a second use for the idea. Large totally real number fields might reopen a conjecture about adding and multiplying real numbers. Their first attempt carried over the unit-distance construction’s requirement for small primes that split in the field. They discovered that units sufficed. A short geometric progression has few products but is too sparse to control sums. They replaced it with a denser collection of units and replaced an interval with a box of algebraic integers. For arbitrarily large finite sets A of real numbers, both the sumset and the product set are bounded by the size of A raised to a fixed power strictly below two. The authors used GPT early as a sounding board and credit GPT-5.5 Pro with an elementary lemma that simplified one step. The idea of carrying this construction from geometry to arithmetic, and the main ideas that made it work, were theirs. <a class="citation" id="cite-132-1" href="#ref-132" aria-label="Source 132">[132]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-133-1" href="#ref-133" aria-label="Source 133">[133]</a> Yichen Huang later gave an April model snapshot the precise real sum-product statement and told it the statement was true. With search and tools disabled, seven of eight runs produced proofs Huang judged correct. Some avoided the use of units central to the human construction. The four authors had recognized a target worth reopening. Huang tested whether a model could prove their statement. The decision to ask for that proof was already in his prompt. <a class="citation" id="cite-152-1" href="#ref-152" aria-label="Source 152">[152]</a></p><p>The direction of the handoff varies. Tony Feng credits Aletheia with connecting arithmetic eigenweights to symmetric-group representation theory and determining them for all classical groups. A machine found a bijection explaining why exchanging two dimensions leaves a family of q,t-Narayana polynomials unchanged. On the Darcy inverse problem, ProofCouncil removed a logarithmic loss from the human upper bound and earned full marks for correctness and exposition from the reviewers in First Proof’s third batch, zero for attribution. The reviewers knew which result had advanced and whose inversion formula it used. Across representation theory, algebraic geometry, abstract algebra, algebraic combinatorics and theoretical PDE, machines are already doing the conceptual work that was supposed to keep them out. <a class="citation" id="cite-1-5" href="#ref-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-3-1" href="#ref-3" aria-label="Source 3">[3]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-8-1" href="#ref-8" aria-label="Source 8">[8]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-16-1" href="#ref-16" aria-label="Source 16">[16]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-99-1" href="#ref-99" aria-label="Source 99">[99]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-162-1" href="#ref-162" aria-label="Source 162">[162]</a></p><p>The October catalogue has no numerical-analysis category and gives no attempted-problem totals by field. Its counts establish reach. They give us no success rate for autonomous sparse PDE research. There is direct evidence about what a model can do once someone supplies the numerical problem. <a class="citation" id="cite-4-1" href="#ref-4" aria-label="Source 4">[4]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-5-1" href="#ref-5" aria-label="Source 5">[5]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-6-1" href="#ref-6" aria-label="Source 6">[6]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-7-1" href="#ref-7" aria-label="Source 7">[7]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-16-2" href="#ref-16" aria-label="Source 16">[16]</a> <a href="#mathematical-and-benchmark-record">Appendix A</a></p><p>Johannes Brust and Tamara Kolda asked how to solve a tensor-fitting system from scattered observations using preconditioned conjugate gradients. Two systems each answered under two prompt conditions, and OpenAI published a fifth solution separately. All five solutions found the same saving in the matrix-vector product. They evaluated only the entries the observations required. The authors incorporated the method and report a further two- to fivefold speedup over their already accelerated solver. They traced the idea to earlier work on sparse kernel learning that none of the answers cited. The researchers supplied the problem. The models found a better way to do the work. Sparsity was no refuge here either. <a class="citation" id="cite-155-1" href="#ref-155" aria-label="Source 155">[155]</a> Give me a solver, a stopwatch and an agreed error tolerance, and I’d try a second agent first. <a class="citation" id="cite-159-1" href="#ref-159" aria-label="Source 159">[159]</a></p></section><section id="why-compression-still-matters"><h2>3. What the margins force and an ansatz proves</h2><h3 id="what-the-margins-of-a-matrix-force">What the margins of a matrix force</h3><p>Richard Brualdi and Jeffrey Ross ask how much of a zero-one matrix can be recovered after only its row and column totals have been saved. Put four ones down the diagonal of a four-by-four matrix. Every row and every column has total one. The upper-left two-by-two block contains two ones. Exchange the first two columns with the last two. The same eight totals now describe a matrix with no ones in that block. Those totals are the degrees of a bipartite graph. The block count asks which rows actually meet which columns. <a class="citation" id="cite-164-1" href="#ref-164" aria-label="Source 164">[164]</a></p><p>Brualdi and Ross proved something much stronger than the example. Take all binary matrices with the specified margins, assuming at least one exists. If every entry can vary somewhere in that family, then every block formed by a nonempty proper subset of the rows and a nonempty proper subset of the columns can vary in its count of ones. Turn it around. If even one such block has a fixed total, the margins must have forced at least one individual entry somewhere in the matrix. The theorem gets a geometric conclusion out of what the margins force throughout the family. <a class="citation" id="cite-164-2" href="#ref-164" aria-label="Source 164">[164]</a></p><p>The margins leave a family of possible matrices. Brualdi and Ross ask which questions have the same answer throughout that family. Zeilberger’s ansatz works backward. Start with the question, then choose an object whose properties could settle it. <a class="citation" id="cite-163-1" href="#ref-163" aria-label="Source 163">[163]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-164-3" href="#ref-164" aria-label="Source 164">[164]</a></p><p>Zeilberger made the choice of what a machine should guess into a research program. He called it the <i>ansatz ansatz</i>. <a class="citation" id="cite-167-1" href="#ref-167" aria-label="Source 167">[167]</a> The determinant example that follows uses a cofactor idea he credited to David Robbins. <a class="citation" id="cite-168-1" href="#ref-168" aria-label="Source 168">[168]</a></p><h3>What the machine should guess</h3><p>George Andrews and David Robbins had conjectured a formula for the weighted count of totally symmetric plane partitions. Okada had reduced the count to a determinant. Koutschan, Kauers and Zeilberger asked what a machine could guess that would prove the formula. They chose the normalized cofactors of the last row. Each cofactor is the signed determinant left after deleting that row and one column. Divide each by the determinant at the preceding size. Nothing guaranteed that the resulting numbers would satisfy recurrences of a kind the computer could discover. Choosing to look for those recurrences was the ansatz. <a class="citation" id="cite-163-2" href="#ref-163" aria-label="Source 163">[163]</a></p><p>The preceding determinant is nonzero, so the earlier rows cut out a line. Setting the last coordinate to one selects a point on it. Pair that vector with the last row and you get the ratio of successive determinants. Small cases gave the computer enough data to guess recurrences for the vector’s entries as the matrix grew. The team then proved that the vector given by the recurrences has last coordinate one, pairs to zero with each earlier row, and gives the conjectured ratio when paired with the last. Induction gave the count at every size. <a class="citation" id="cite-163-3" href="#ref-163" aria-label="Source 163">[163]</a></p><p>The verification had a geometry of its own. Put the shift monomial <i>S</i><sub>n</sub><sup>a</sup><i>S</i><sub>j</sub><sup>b</sup> at the lattice point (a,b). A Gröbner basis gives rules for reducing these shifts. A leading monomial at (2,1), for instance, would give a rule for every shift with a ≥ 2 and b ≥ 1. The points left over form a staircase. <a href="#ref-163">Koutschan</a> restricted the correction terms in the certificate to those shifts and solved for their coefficients. Summing the resulting identity over the column index makes the correction telescope, leaving a recurrence for the sum. On the <a href="#four-triangles-and-two-straight-lines">Stokes mesh below</a>, two straight lines will restrict the space of possible divergences by forcing an alternating sum to vanish.</p><figure class="figure"><svg class="groebner-staircase-diagram" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 240 122" role="img" aria-label="Illustrative Gröbner staircase on the exponent lattice. The point (2,1) is boxed. Crosses mark points at or to its right and at or above it. Open circles mark the other displayed points." focusable="false" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:240px;margin:auto" fill="none" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M25 8L25 107 M25 107L222 107 M21 12L25 8 M25 8L29 12 M218 103L222 107 M222 107L218 111" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.2"/><g fill="var(--ink)" font-size="12" font-family="Georgia,serif" text-anchor="middle"><text x="43" y="117">0</text><text x="83" y="117">1</text><text x="123" y="117">2</text><text x="163" y="117">3</text><text x="203" y="117">4</text><text x="15" y="99">0</text><text x="15" y="75">1</text><text x="15" y="51">2</text><text x="15" y="27">3</text><text x="232" y="111" font-style="italic">a</text><text x="14" y="13" font-style="italic">b</text></g><g data-a="0" data-b="0" data-reducible="false"><circle cx="43" cy="95" r="3.2" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="1" data-b="0" data-reducible="false"><circle cx="83" cy="95" r="3.2" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="2" data-b="0" data-reducible="false"><circle cx="123" cy="95" r="3.2" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="3" data-b="0" data-reducible="false"><circle cx="163" cy="95" r="3.2" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="4" data-b="0" data-reducible="false"><circle cx="203" cy="95" r="3.2" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="0" data-b="1" data-reducible="false"><circle cx="43" cy="71" r="3.2" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="1" data-b="1" data-reducible="false"><circle cx="83" cy="71" r="3.2" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="2" data-b="1" data-reducible="true"><path d="M120 68l6 6m0 -6l-6 6" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="115" y="63" width="16" height="16" rx="2" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="3" data-b="1" data-reducible="true"><path d="M160 68l6 6m0 -6l-6 6" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="1.6"/></g><g data-a="4" data-b="1" data-reducible="true"><path d="M200 68l6 6m0 -6l-6 6" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="1.6"/></g><g data-a="0" data-b="2" data-reducible="false"><circle cx="43" cy="47" r="3.2" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="1" data-b="2" data-reducible="false"><circle cx="83" cy="47" r="3.2" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="2" data-b="2" data-reducible="true"><path d="M120 44l6 6m0 -6l-6 6" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="1.6"/></g><g data-a="3" data-b="2" data-reducible="true"><path d="M160 44l6 6m0 -6l-6 6" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="1.6"/></g><g data-a="4" data-b="2" data-reducible="true"><path d="M200 44l6 6m0 -6l-6 6" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="1.6"/></g><g data-a="0" data-b="3" data-reducible="false"><circle cx="43" cy="23" r="3.2" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="1" data-b="3" data-reducible="false"><circle cx="83" cy="23" r="3.2" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.25"/></g><g data-a="2" data-b="3" data-reducible="true"><path d="M120 20l6 6m0 -6l-6 6" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="1.6"/></g><g data-a="3" data-b="3" data-reducible="true"><path d="M160 20l6 6m0 -6l-6 6" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="1.6"/></g><g data-a="4" data-b="3" data-reducible="true"><path d="M200 20l6 6m0 -6l-6 6" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="1.6"/></g></svg><figcaption>The box marks the illustrative leading shift at (2,1). Crosses mark shifts reducible by this rule, where a ≥ 2 and b ≥ 1. Open circles survive this rule. The full staircase uses all the leading shifts.</figcaption></figure><p>By 1998, Zeilberger had built a machine that could find counting schemes for itself. His empirical program HERB helped him design the rigorous search WILF. When WILF exhausted its depth allowance, it returned zero. That meant that no scheme had been found within the rules. A deeper search could still succeed. A useful certificate has to say what a pass proves and what a failed search leaves open. Every later user of WILF inherited a piece of Zeilberger’s mathematical judgment. <a class="citation" id="cite-82-1" href="#ref-82" aria-label="Source 82">[82]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-117-1" href="#ref-117" aria-label="Source 117">[117]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-121-1" href="#ref-121" aria-label="Source 121">[121]</a></p><p>In <a href="https://refactor.press/essays/continuity-as-organized-discrete-data#choosing-how-far-to-look">Continuity as Organized Discrete Data</a>, every row of a table starts with ones and ends in zeros. Row m has exactly m ones. Every row represents zero in the limit. Pick its mth entry and the diagonal is all ones. The limit was the right object for identifying the value of each row. To use the rows together we need to know how far along each one to look. That move is worth learning. Ask what the old identification licensed, exhibit the next operation on which it fails, and recover just enough information to perform that operation. Sometimes the result is a schedule for a calculation. Sometimes it is a new mathematical object.</p><p>In my own <a href="https://refactor.press/papers/taiko-girth-obstruction">Taiko product structures</a>, translating a group-ring construction into incidence geometry exposed intersection constraints that the coarse counts concealed. On the next mesh those intersections can be literal. Each new mass is a sum over the overlaps of two grids. The question is which other sums those overlaps still let us calculate. <a class="citation" id="cite-80-1" href="#ref-80" aria-label="Source 80">[80]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-147-3" href="#ref-147" aria-label="Source 147">[147]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-158-1" href="#ref-158" aria-label="Source 158">[158]</a></p></section><section id="testing-the-proposed-advantage"><h2>4. What will the next question require us to save?</h2><p>I gave agents two fields, numerical PDE and sumsets. They found two ways for a correct record to lose its grip on the next question. On the mesh, recovering a regional mass can cost precision as well as storage. On the ruler, changing the kind of comparison we allow can halve the number of questions. <a class="citation" id="cite-151-4" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a></p><h3>What a conservative mesh forgets</h3><p>Return to the ring and take an even number n of equal cells. The half-cell averages lose exactly one direction, the alternating pattern. A window covering two adjacent old cells gets its total from one shifted average. Every even-length window can be tiled by such pairs. For an odd number of whole old cells and concentrations between zero and one, the sharp worst-case error in the mass is 1/(2n). Compatible fields differ by a multiple of the alternating pattern. The two checkerboards attain the full ambiguity of 1/n, and the midpoint of the compatible range halves it. Saving one alternating sum restores every old cell average. It buys nothing for the even windows. <a class="citation" id="cite-151-5" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a></p><p>Move the grid by a little more than half a cell. If the extra shift is a fraction d of a cell, with 0 &lt; d &lt; 1/2, the average of neighbors u and v becomes (1/2 − d)u + (1/2 + d)v. The alternating mode now survives with multiplier −2d. Recovering it amplifies an error in that mode by 1/(2d). An exact inverse can still be useless at the precision of the observations. Numerical analysis is an especially good home for that question because the receiving calculation can impose its own units, tolerance and cost. Karl Taylor gives a practical instance. In his coarse-grid temperature example, two remaps preserve the same global mean. Their area-weighted root-mean-square errors are 0.15 K and 0.63 K. The one with the smaller error also exceeds the source maximum. A collaborator who needs a bound on extremes has a real reason to demand another observation. <a class="citation" id="cite-147-4" href="#ref-147" aria-label="Source 147">[147]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-153-1" href="#ref-153" aria-label="Source 153">[153]</a></p><p>On an irregular mesh the underlying geometry is literal. Intersect each old cell with each new cell. If the concentration is constant inside each old cell, multiply that concentration by the overlap volume and sum to get the mass in a new cell. Farrell and Maddison use a common subdivision for conservative projection in two and three dimensions. Croci and Farrell prove that on two quasi-uniform meshes, with bounded subdivision per intersecting pair, the number of pieces grows at most linearly in the combined cell count. That prices the forward construction. It leaves open which regional masses the new measurements determine. <a class="citation" id="cite-147-5" href="#ref-147" aria-label="Source 147">[147]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-158-2" href="#ref-158" aria-label="Source 158">[158]</a></p><p>Brualdi and Ross asked which block totals survive when a binary matrix is replaced by its margins. On a mesh the overlap volumes are known and the old concentrations are the unknowns. Here the return question has an exact answer for one transfer. Write O for the matrix of overlap volumes and u for the old cell concentrations. The new masses are Ou. For a requested region, let w record how much of each old cell it covers. Its mass is the weighted sum of u with weights w. We can recover that mass for every old field exactly when w is a linear combination of the rows of O. The coefficients of that combination tell us how to add the new masses. If there is no such combination, some invisible perturbation changes the requested mass. Take small opposite multiples of it around half concentration and both fields remain between zero and one. They produce the same new masses. <a class="citation" id="cite-164-4" href="#ref-164" aria-label="Source 164">[164]</a></p><p>Now allow error in the readings. Two fields can be compatible with the same reported reading even if their exact measurements differ. Take the largest difference in regional mass among all such pairs. Any answer must miss by at least half that difference on one field of a maximizing pair. Conversely, for each reported reading, estimate the mass by the midpoint of its compatible range. So half the largest difference is exactly the best worst-case error. <a href="#optimal-regional-mass-estimate">Appendix B</a></p><p>For one specified region, with exact overlaps and independent interval bounds on the donor concentrations and measurement errors, a linear program finds an estimator that attains this limit. This is a case of Ettehad and Foucart’s optimal-recovery theorem. Start with the mass of a uniform half-concentration field. Add a weighted sum of the deviations of the readings from that field’s readings. The error comes from two places. The overlap rows can fail to represent part of the regional weights, and the reconstruction coefficients can amplify reading errors. An exact representation can demand more precision than the simulation stores. Giving up some exactness in the weights can reduce the worst-case error. Even a nonlinear reconstruction cannot improve on the optimum under these assumptions. The guarantee covers the represented field. A continuum claim also needs a bound on variation inside the donor cells. With several transfers, the maps compose after each intermediate mass is divided by its cell volume. <a href="#optimal-regional-mass-estimate">Appendix B</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-147-6" href="#ref-147" aria-label="Source 147">[147]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-151-6" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-153-2" href="#ref-153" aria-label="Source 153">[153]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-166-1" href="#ref-166" aria-label="Source 166">[166]</a></p><h3>What a complete sumset table forgets</h3><p>Take the five marks 0, 1, 2, 6, 22 on a ruler. On a second ruler move only the 6 to 7. Form all sums of two marks, allowing repeats. The rulers agree on every labelled comparison, including the tie 0 + 2 = 1 + 1. Each has fourteen distinct sums. Now form sums of three marks. On the first ruler 2 + 2 + 2 = 0 + 0 + 6. On the second, the corresponding sums separate. There are now 29 distinct sums on the first and 30 on the second. The full old table was a richer description than the count, and even it stopped one operation short. <a class="citation" id="cite-151-7" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a></p><p>Hide the inner marks. Tell the observer only the common two-sum order, the diameter 22, and that the five integer marks start at zero and have greatest common divisor one. Seven rulers fit. Their three-sum counts are 28, 29 or 30. Allow the observer to choose two labelled three-sums and ask whether they are equal. Four questions are necessary and sufficient in the worst case. Across the seven rulers the varying tests give only four different patterns of answers. Each singles out a different group with fewer than 30 sums. On a ruler with 30, all four answers are no, and each possible exception has to be excluded. Let the same question return less, equal or greater and two suffice.</p><p>Write the marks as 0, a, b, c, 22. First compare 3c with b + 22. Equality gives 29. If 3c is smaller, compare 3b with c. Greater, equal and smaller give counts of 28, 29 and 30 respectively. If 3c is greater, compare b + 2c with 22. Equality gives 29 and a smaller sum gives 30. Those are the only possibilities in that branch. Pad shorter sums with zeros so every test compares two labelled three-sums. No one comparison settles all seven rulers. The accompanying calculation checks all 595 pairs. The sign tells us which question to ask next. <a class="citation" id="cite-151-8" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a></p><p>The sumset successor has an exact demand of its own. In my preprint a face of a polytope records every labelled order and tie through a chosen number of summands. <a class="citation" id="cite-145-1" href="#ref-145" aria-label="Source 145">[145]</a> Its geometry distinguishes a new equality from a change of order. The three-sum count cares whether values merge. A change of order by itself can leave the count untouched. Which of the possible new equalities can change the count on a whole family of rulers? Keep five marks and increase the number of summands. Can the geometry of the old table bound the number of new signed comparisons needed for the next count? Which tables attain that bound? I have used the same habit in polynomial fibers. I wanted to count integer inputs x for which f(x) = g(y) has a rational lift beyond those supplied by polynomial composition. Given the effective curve operations in the paper, geometry and finite congruence tests decide whether that count has a power term. Listing every exceptional input would be a stronger demand. <a class="citation" id="cite-146-1" href="#ref-146" aria-label="Source 146">[146]</a> These papers establish that I can make this move. They are part of the rival team’s reading list as well. How much my presence would add is a separate question.</p><p>The machine rival belongs in this very field. Tim Gowers reports that both Isaac Rajagopal and ChatGPT discussed the prospect of achieving polynomial diameter. Gowers asked the model to pursue it. Rajagopal describes the mathematical brief as replacing his geometric progressions with smaller sets having the requisite pattern of sumset sizes. ChatGPT paired carefully chosen numbers with their multiples. The pairs create the wanted collisions while the generators exclude accidental short relations. Rajagopal worked out the bound for fixed sumset order and sufficiently many marks. The machine helped recognize the opportunity and invented the representation. In the two cases here, agents chose the examples, identified the lost observations and found the two-question sumset procedure. <a class="citation" id="cite-145-2" href="#ref-145" aria-label="Source 145">[145]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-148-1" href="#ref-148" aria-label="Source 148">[148]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-151-9" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a></p><p>The ruler tells us how many exact comparisons we need. On the mesh, we can ask how many bits it takes to recover the answer at the scientist’s tolerance. I would start with a tracer on triangular meshes adapted to its front in a rectangular channel, remapped to a coarser output grid. The receiving scientist names a short list of observation patches in physical coordinates, with boundaries that can cut across either grid, and one absolute tolerance τ for their masses. Which patch she will ask about is settled after the old grid is gone. I would try to prove matching upper and lower bounds on the extra bits needed to protect every patch on the list.</p><p>Allow masses of individual donor cells and of connected groups of up to four edge-adjacent donor cells. With the grids given, choose the groups and precisions from their geometry and the patch list before inspecting the concentrations. Store each mass in physical units, rounded to fixed-point levels over its declared range. Hold the precision of the coarser record fixed and count all extra bits, including patch identifiers, precision choices and old-grid geometry the reader needs. Compare with keeping just the remap and with keeping a quantized donor snapshot at the same tolerance, including its geometry. The units and range matter. Rescaling a measurement while holding its absolute error and price fixed can create an apparent precision gain out of nothing. <a class="citation" id="cite-147-7" href="#ref-147" aria-label="Source 147">[147]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-151-10" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-153-3" href="#ref-153" aria-label="Source 153">[153]</a></p><p>If the query family includes every old cell and the tolerance is zero, the augmented measurements must have full column rank. For the specified patches and positive tolerance, a construction must protect every allowed query on the stated mesh family. To rule out a budget of B bits, take each permitted design using at most B bits and exhibit two fields with the same complete stored record whose masses differ by more than 2τ on some allowed patch. The pair and the patch can depend on the design. With deterministic rounding, the pair must produce identical codes. That lower bound would change what the scientist has to request before the next run. <a class="citation" id="cite-147-8" href="#ref-147" aria-label="Source 147">[147]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-151-11" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-153-4" href="#ref-153" aria-label="Source 153">[153]</a></p><p>The graph gives the complementary case. The full operator is still available. A representation built for one job can hide an error from the coarse calculation even when the original equations can detect it.</p></section><section id="structure-under-computation"><h2>5. What a sparse graph can afford to forget</h2><p>Start with a finite weighted graph. Orient its edges, let B be the edge-vertex incidence matrix with -1 at each edge&#x27;s tail and +1 at its head, and put its positive edge weights in the diagonal matrix W. The graph Laplacian L has the following quadratic form.</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="L = B^T W B,     x^T L x = sum_{edges {i,j}} w_ij (x_i - x_j)^2."><i>L</i> = <i>B</i><sup>T</sup><i>WB</i>, &nbsp; <i>x</i><sup>T</sup><i>Lx</i> = Σ<sub>edges {i,j}</sub> <i>w</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>x</i><sub>i</sub> - <i>x</i><sub>j</sub>)<sup>2</sup>.</div><p>For a connected graph, the constant vectors form the kernel. Ground a vertex or work on the mean-zero subspace to obtain a positive-definite problem. We will also use a positive diagonal shift. In either case we know exactly which quantity a change of representation must respect. <a class="citation" id="cite-19-1" href="#ref-19" aria-label="Source 19">[19]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-26-1" href="#ref-26" aria-label="Source 26">[26]</a></p><p>Elimination makes the central difficulty visible. Suppose a vertex is joined to three neighbors by edges of weight one. For fixed neighbor values, minimize the energy over the central value. The minimizer is their average. Substitution leaves exactly the energy of a triangle on the three neighbors, each new edge having weight one third. For a star with positive incident-weight vector a, elimination gives the Schur complement below.</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="S = diag(a) - a a^T / s,     s = sum_i a_i;     a = (1,1,1)^T gives triangle weights 1/3."><i>S</i> = diag(<i>a</i>) - <i>aa</i><sup>T</sup>/<i>s</i>, &nbsp; <i>s</i> = Σ<sub>i</sub><i>a</i><sub>i</sub>.<br/>For <i>a</i> = (1,1,1)<sup>T</sup>, every new edge has weight 1/3.</div><figure class="figure"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 640 205" role="img" aria-labelledby="diagram-title diagram-desc"><title id="diagram-title">Eliminating the center of a unit-weight star</title><desc id="diagram-desc">Three neighbors each join a center with weight one. Eliminating the center connects the three neighbors in a triangle with edge weight one third, preserving minimized energy.</desc><g stroke="#aabbbf" stroke-width="2" fill="none"><path d="M115 100L40 35M115 100L40 165M115 100L200 100"/><path d="M425 35L425 165L565 100Z"/><path d="M255 100H350m-10-7 10 7-10 7"/></g><g fill="#10212a" stroke="#c8edb3" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="115" cy="100" r="19"/><circle cx="40" cy="35" r="13"/><circle cx="40" cy="165" r="13"/><circle cx="200" cy="100" r="13"/><circle cx="425" cy="35" r="13"/><circle cx="425" cy="165" r="13"/><circle cx="565" cy="100" r="13"/></g><g fill="#f0eee5" font-size="17" font-family="Georgia,serif" text-anchor="middle"><text x="115" y="106">v</text><text x="40" y="18">1</text><text x="40" y="196">2</text><text x="221" y="106">3</text><text x="425" y="18">1</text><text x="425" y="196">2</text><text x="590" y="106">3</text><text x="65" y="86">1</text><text x="64" y="128">1</text><text x="158" y="88">1</text><text x="405" y="106">1/3</text><text x="501" y="56">1/3</text><text x="501" y="154">1/3</text><text x="300" y="79">Eliminate v</text></g></svg><figcaption>Eliminating the center of a three-edge unit star produces a triangle with edge weights 1/3. For any fixed values at the three remaining vertices, the triangle has exactly the star&#x27;s minimum energy over the eliminated central value. Both retain the constant-vector nullspace.</figcaption></figure><p>We can discard the central value because the question asked for the minimum energy at each choice of boundary values. The triangle gives exactly that answer. Repeat the elimination and previously unconnected variables can become coupled, creating fill. Retaining every new interaction can destroy the economy of a sparse input. Discarding interactions changes the energy that made the elimination correct. Spectral sparsification seeks a much smaller graph whose energy approximates the original for every vector. Effective-resistance sampling assigns importance using the surrounding network as well as the edge weight. <a class="citation" id="cite-19-2" href="#ref-19" aria-label="Source 19">[19]</a></p><p>Batson, Spielman and Srivastava show that a reweighted subgraph with at most ceil((n − 1)/ε²) edges can preserve every vector’s energy between factors (1 − ε)² and (1 + ε)² for 0 &lt; ε &lt; 1. This is what justifies forgetting edges. The four-cycle that comes next uses another kind of compression. Relaxation removes the errors it can shrink cheaply and leaves the rest to a coarse space. Which errors belong there depends on the actual operator. <a class="citation" id="cite-20-1" href="#ref-20" aria-label="Source 20">[20]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-84-1" href="#ref-84" aria-label="Source 84">[84]</a></p><p>Now hand the analyst a proposed solution and ask how accurate it is. In this two-variable system an entire unit of error in the second variable barely registers in the residual.</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="A = diag(1, 10^-8),  b = (1, 10^-8)^T,  x = (1,1)^T,  y = (1,0)^T."><i>A</i> = diag(1, 10<sup>-8</sup>), &nbsp; <i>b</i> = (1, 10<sup>-8</sup>)<sup>T</sup>,<br/><i>x</i> = (1,1)<sup>T</sup>, &nbsp; <i>y</i> = (1,0)<sup>T</sup>.</div><p>The relative residual in the infinity norm is 10<sup>-8</sup>, while the relative forward error in the same norm is 1. The proposed answer loses the entire second component. Interpreting a residual therefore requires conditioning information and a specified error measure. LAPACK&#x27;s error analysis makes that distinction explicit. <a class="citation" id="cite-21-1" href="#ref-21" aria-label="Source 21">[21]</a></p><p>A more discriminating inexpensive observation exposes this particular failure. With r = b - A y and absolute values taken entrywise, the componentwise backward error is</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="omega_c = max_i |r_i| / (|A| |y| + |b|)_i = 1.">ω<sub>c</sub> = max<sub><i>i</i></sub> |<i>r</i><sub>i</sub>| / (|<i>A</i>| |<i>y</i>| + |<i>b</i>|)<sub>i</sub> = 1.</div><p>Its denominator is (2, 10<sup>-8</sup>), so the lost second component remains visible. Computing this quantity needs a sparse matrix-vector product, without an inverse. Forward-error control still requires the appropriate conditioning information. The defect was visible in the residual all along. Dividing by a single global scale was the decision that hid it. <a class="citation" id="cite-21-2" href="#ref-21" aria-label="Source 21">[21]</a></p><p>Even a perfect componentwise check certifies only the equations put into the machine. Where did those equations come from? In scalar elliptic finite elements, Boman, Hendrickson and Vavasis join the nodes of each element in a weighted star. They prove that its assembled graph energy is comparable to the stiffness energy. This lets a graph preconditioner accelerate the original finite-element solve. The bound depends on element shape, quadrature and conductivity variation within an element. A large jump along element boundaries can be harmless to the comparison. Move it across an element and it enters the bound. To say how close the computed field is to the continuum solution still takes an approximation estimate. Each passage earns a different conclusion. <a class="citation" id="cite-21-3" href="#ref-21" aria-label="Source 21">[21]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-52-1" href="#ref-52" aria-label="Source 52">[52]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-156-1" href="#ref-156" aria-label="Source 156">[156]</a></p></section><section id="a-concrete-research-direction"><h2>6. When new weights or geometry defeat the solver</h2><p>Nothing has to move for a solver to miss an error. On a four-vertex cycle with alternating strong and weak edges, a solver groups the vertices joined by each strong edge so it can correct errors shared by the pair. Now exchange the weights while keeping the old pairs. The matrix has the same size, the same pattern of nonzeros, the same diagonal, even the same eigenvalues. Give both vertices of one new strong pair error +1 and both vertices of the other error −1. The residual sums to zero on each old pair. From those two sums alone, the coarse solver sees exactly what it would see if the answer were already right.</p><p>To see why the weight exchange matters, follow the error through the smoother and the coarse correction. For a symmetric positive-definite matrix A and a full-column-rank matrix P of coarse directions, the exact correction is</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="A_c = P^T A P,     x_new = x + P A_c^(-1) P^T (b - A x)."><i>A</i><sub>c</sub> = <i>P</i><sup>T</sup><i>AP</i>,<br/><i>x</i><sub>new</sub> = <i>x</i> + <i>PA</i><sub>c</sub><sup>-1</sup><i>P</i><sup>T</sup>(<i>b</i> - <i>Ax</i>).</div><p>Write e for the exact solution minus the current iterate. Then the exact coarse correction removes every vector in the column space of P and leaves every vector with <i>P</i><sup>T</sup> A v = 0 untouched. Its error-propagation operator is</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="e_new = E_c e,     E_c = I - P (P^T A P)^(-1) P^T A."><i>e</i><sub>new</sub> = <i>E</i><sub>c</sub><i>e</i>,<br/><i>E</i><sub>c</sub> = <i>I</i> - <i>P</i>(<i>P</i><sup>T</sup><i>AP</i>)<sup>-1</sup><i>P</i><sup>T</sup><i>A</i>.</div><p>Return to the four-cycle 1-2-3-4-1. Let A = L + ε I, with 0 &lt; ε &lt; 1. Give edges 12 and 34 weight one and the other edges weight ε. The two columns of P indicate the pairs {1,2} and {3,4}. Exchange the strong and weak edges to obtain A′. A relabeling takes A to A′, so the spectrum, diagonal and sparsity pattern all agree. The diagram follows the same error v = (1,−1,−1,1)<sup>T</sup> through that exchange.</p><figure class="figure"><div class="table-scroll"><svg style="display:block;width:100%;min-width:620px" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 760 400" role="img" aria-labelledby="four-cycle-title four-cycle-description">
