</div><figcaption>The shaded regions retain the original aggregates {1,2} and {3,4}. Thick edges have weight 1 and thin edges weight epsilon. After the weights are exchanged, the missed vector has constant sign on the new strong pairs {1,4} and {2,3}, but opposite signs within each retained aggregate. The diagonal shift epsilon I is present in both operators.</figcaption></figure><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="A&#x27; v = 3 epsilon v,     P^T A&#x27; v = 0."><i>A</i>′<i>v</i> = 3ε<i>v</i>, &nbsp; <i>P</i><sup>T</sup><i>A</i>′<i>v</i> = 0.</div><p>The old pairs see zero coarse residual on both graphs. What changes is the work the coarse level must do. Before the exchange, Av = (2 + ε)v. Afterward, A′v = 3εv. The common diagonal is (1 + 2ε)I, so a weighted Jacobi step on a mode of eigenvalue λ multiplies it by 1 − ωλ/(1 + 2ε). With ω = 2/3 and small ε, the original multiplier approaches −1/3 and the new one approaches 1. The coarse space missed this error all along. Before the exchange the smoother could do most of the work on it in one step. Now almost all that work falls to the coarse space that cannot see it.</p><p>Grouping {1,4} and {2,3} puts v in the new coarse space. One exact coarse correction then removes it. On an unfamiliar graph we need an affordable way of discovering <a href="https://refactor.press/essays/when-a-description-becomes-a-verdict#enough-for-which-question">which distinction the retained description misses</a>. Adaptive smoothed aggregation and compatible relaxation already generate or test coarse directions. Bao, Cui and Li learn a coarse space from the errors the smoother leaves behind. Their sharper measure compares spaces regardless of which basis represents them. The Gram matrix for that comparison is small. Building it takes solves with the original matrix, so they use it offline. Can we get enough of the same information while a four-solve job is still worth saving? <a class="citation" id="cite-20-2" href="#ref-20" aria-label="Source 20">[20]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-78-1" href="#ref-78" aria-label="Source 78">[78]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-79-1" href="#ref-79" aria-label="Source 79">[79]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-86-1" href="#ref-86" aria-label="Source 86">[86]</a></p><p>A changed workload can reward a different decision for a second reason. In the CAST elimination study, CAST-1 won on the third Chimera graph with four solves per factor and CAST-2 won at 250. On the other four graphs CAST-2 was still slower at 250. A solver needs to know which change mattered. Sampling more edges can improve a local error estimate and increase the fill at later eliminations. <a class="citation" id="cite-48-1" href="#ref-48" aria-label="Source 48">[48]</a> <a href="#solver-evidence-and-extensions">Appendix B</a> works through the timing and an exact triangle on which the mean graph conceals the error in every sample.</p><h3>Which promises survive a change?</h3><p>Some changes admit an inexpensive certificate. Let A = L(w) + μ I and A&#x27; = L(w&#x27;) + μ I on the same graph, with positive edge weights and fixed μ &gt; 0. Choose 0 &lt; c ≤ 1 ≤ C so that every edge&#x27;s new weight divided by its old weight lies between c and C. Comparing the edge energies and the common grounding term gives the first inequality below. For a fixed symmetric positive-definite preconditioner M with an already proved comparison α M ≤ A ≤ β M, transitivity gives the second.</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="c A &lt;= A&#x27; &lt;= C A,     c alpha M &lt;= A&#x27; &lt;= C beta M."><i>cA</i> ≤ <i>A</i>′ ≤ <i>CA</i>,<br/><i>c</i>α<i>M</i> ≤ <i>A</i>′ ≤ <i>C</i>β<i>M</i>.</div><p>One exact-arithmetic pass through the weight ratios gives a condition-number bound Cβ/(cα) for the fixed M on the new system. A small group of extreme changes can make those global constants pessimistic. Updating level matrices or smoothers changes M and requires fresh analysis. A floating-point certificate also needs rounding control. <a href="#experimental-protocol">Appendix C</a> compares keeping the old hierarchy intact with updating its matrices or rebuilding it. <a class="citation" id="cite-19-3" href="#ref-19" aria-label="Source 19">[19]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-52-2" href="#ref-52" aria-label="Source 52">[52]</a></p><h3 id="four-triangles-and-two-straight-lines">Four triangles and two straight lines</h3><p>Incompressible Stokes flow gives a sharper reason to care about geometry. Draw four triangles around an interior vertex, with the four edges through it lying on two straight lines. Number the triangles in cyclic order. For a continuous piecewise-polynomial velocity, let q₁ through q₄ be its four one-sided divergence values at the vertex. Then q₁ − q₂ + q₃ − q₄ = 0. Continuity along an edge makes the two tangential derivatives there agree. On two straight lines those agreements cancel in the alternating sum. A pressure space meant to equal the range of the discrete divergence has to remember that relation. The abstract mesh graph cannot tell whether the two pairs of edges are straight. <a class="citation" id="cite-140-1" href="#ref-140" aria-label="Source 140">[140]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-141-1" href="#ref-141" aria-label="Source 141">[141]</a></p><figure class="figure"><div class="stokes-alignment-diagram" role="img" aria-label="Two four-triangle patches with the same outer vertices. At left, the highlighted north-south edges lie on one straight line. At right, the center has moved one tenth of a unit east of the dashed reference line and the highlighted edges bend. For the velocity in the worked example, the alternating divergence sum changes from zero to two ninths." style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:.35rem .9rem;max-width:29rem;margin:auto;text-align:center;font:.91rem/1.25 var(--sans)"><strong>Aligned</strong><strong>Center moved east</strong><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 160 156" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:172px;margin:auto" fill="none" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="80,14 144,78 80,142 16,78 80,14" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.5"/><polyline points="16,78 144,78" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.5"/><polyline points="80,14 80,78 80,142" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="14" r="2.7" fill="var(--ink)"/><circle cx="144" cy="78" r="2.7" fill="var(--ink)"/><circle cx="80" cy="142" r="2.7" fill="var(--ink)"/><circle cx="16" cy="78" r="2.7" fill="var(--ink)"/><circle cx="80" cy="78" r="3.7" fill="var(--refactor-mint)"/></svg><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 160 156" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:172px;margin:auto" fill="none" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="80,14 144,78 80,142 16,78 80,14" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.5"/><polyline points="16,78 144,78" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1.5"/><polyline points="80,14 80,142" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><polyline points="80,14 86.4,78 80,142" stroke="var(--refactor-mint)" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="14" r="2.7" fill="var(--ink)"/><circle cx="144" cy="78" r="2.7" fill="var(--ink)"/><circle cx="80" cy="142" r="2.7" fill="var(--ink)"/><circle cx="16" cy="78" r="2.7" fill="var(--ink)"/><circle cx="86.4" cy="78" r="3.7" fill="var(--refactor-mint)"/></svg><span>Alternating sum<br/><strong>0</strong></span><span>Alternating sum<br/><strong>2/9</strong></span></div><figcaption>Moving the center one tenth of a unit east breaks the highlighted north-south alignment. The four outer vertices stay fixed and the east-west pair remains straight. For the nodal velocities in the calculation below, the alternating divergence sum changes from 0 to 2/9.</figcaption></figure><p>Here is an exact check. Put the four outer vertices one unit east, north, west and south of the origin. Give the east vertex unit upward velocity and the other vertices, including the center, zero velocity. Interpolate linearly on each triangle. All four divergences vanish. Move only the center one tenth of a unit east, keeping the nodal velocities.</p><p>Starting with the northeast triangle and going counterclockwise, the divergences are now 1/9, 0, 0 and −1/9. Their alternating sum is 2/9. An incidence relation has disappeared while every edge still joins the same pair of vertices. On nearly aligned meshes the pressure mode that exact alignment would exclude can be poorly controlled. Gräßle, Bohne and Sauter impose the relation at nearly singular vertices as well. For their planar elements of velocity degree at least four, a fixed positive threshold gives a stability bound robust to near-alignment. The price is that the discrete velocity can have nonzero divergence. They bound that defect. <a class="citation" id="cite-140-2" href="#ref-140" aria-label="Source 140">[140]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-141-2" href="#ref-141" aria-label="Source 141">[141]</a></p><p>On a moving mesh the residual can go to zero at every step. Seeing a pressure constraint in those two lines tells us what to ask as they come apart. How close to alignment can we still control that pressure mode? Can we exclude that pressure mode and keep the divergence defect within the tolerance this calculation needs? These are the estimates that should drive a change of mesh or finite-element space. On an unfamiliar mesh, I would want a specialist to spot the relation that tells us what to prove.</p><p>The eddy-current equations need a different incidence relation. Interpolate a nodal field and take its discrete gradient, or take the gradient first and interpolate the edge field. The two paths must agree. Tuminaro and Glusa exhibit a coarse stencil whose allowed edges are disconnected. No choice of interpolation weights on those edges can make the paths agree. Adding edges repairs the obstruction. Their algorithm already does this. A fresh question is whether a transfer that satisfies the identity can also retain the low-energy modes cheaply as material coefficients change. <a class="citation" id="cite-25-1" href="#ref-25" aria-label="Source 25">[25]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-43-1" href="#ref-43" aria-label="Source 43">[43]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-54-1" href="#ref-54" aria-label="Source 54">[54]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-78-2" href="#ref-78" aria-label="Source 78">[78]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-84-2" href="#ref-84" aria-label="Source 84">[84]</a></p><p>A finer mesh can give stronger evidence for a false conclusion. In Arnold, Falk and Winther’s square-domain Maxwell example, continuous piecewise-linear vector fields on a crisscross mesh give seven positive eigenvalues converging to the right values. The eighth converges to a spurious value. Their alternative uses degrees of freedom adapted to the differential operators. For the mixed source problem, a discrete subcomplex and uniformly bounded projections that commute with differentiation give stability. Approximation and further spectral convergence hypotheses let the discrete calculation answer the continuum eigenvalue question. A correct incidence identity starts that argument. The estimates carry it back. <a class="citation" id="cite-22-1" href="#ref-22" aria-label="Source 22">[22]</a></p><p>On the dye ring, the alternating signs picked out a change in the old field that every new average missed. At the aligned Stokes vertex, the same signs give a sum that vanishes on every divergence the patch can produce. Shift the new grid a little farther and the lost dye pattern reappears with so small a multiplier that recovering it amplifies measurement error. Break the Stokes alignment slightly and the extra pressure mode can return before we can control it well. The geometry tells us which direction matters. The estimate tells us what accuracy we can promise.</p><p>For the relation to point-curve incidence and wave-packet estimates, see <a href="#incidence-and-numerical-pde">Appendix B</a>. <a class="citation" id="cite-22-2" href="#ref-22" aria-label="Source 22">[22]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-43-2" href="#ref-43" aria-label="Source 43">[43]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-140-3" href="#ref-140" aria-label="Source 140">[140]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-141-3" href="#ref-141" aria-label="Source 141">[141]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-142-1" href="#ref-142" aria-label="Source 142">[142]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-144-1" href="#ref-144" aria-label="Source 144">[144]</a></p></section><section id="earning-reliability"><h2>7. What makes a new test decisive</h2><p>The four-cycle showed an error that vanished from the coarse residual. We can test the answer against the original system. For symmetric positive-definite A and M, suppose we have proved αM ≤ A with α &gt; 0. Given an approximation y to Ax = b, compute its residual r = b − Ay. Reversing the matrix inequality under inversion gives</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="||x - y||_A^2 = r^T A^(-1) r &lt;= (1/alpha) r^T M^(-1) r.">||<i>x</i> - <i>y</i>||<sub>A</sub><sup>2</sup> = <i>r</i><sup>T</sup><i>A</i><sup>-1</sup><i>r</i> ≤ (1/α) <i>r</i><sup>T</sup><i>M</i><sup>-1</sup><i>r</i>.</div><p>This bounds the energy error. When solving with M is cheap and α is large enough to give a useful bound, the certificate is worth computing. After a weight change, use cα from Section 6 and recompute r against A′. For singular Laplacians, restrict the comparison to the orthogonal complement of the common kernel. An estimated spectral comparison, an approximate solve with M, and rounding in the residual and quadratic form each require control before the calculated number can certify the answer. <a class="citation" id="cite-21-4" href="#ref-21" aria-label="Source 21">[21]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-52-3" href="#ref-52" aria-label="Source 52">[52]</a></p><p>The shifted-Laplacian pilot also admits a conservative check independent of the preconditioner. Since A&#x27; ≥ μ I, any validated upper bound ρ on the Euclidean norm of r = b - A&#x27; y gives</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="||x - y||_A&#x27; &lt;= ||r||_2 / sqrt(mu) &lt;= rho / sqrt(mu).">||<i>x</i> - <i>y</i>||<sub>A′</sub> ≤ ||<i>r</i>||<sub>2</sub>/√μ ≤ ρ/√μ.</div><p>For the shifted graph, a validated norm bound ρ thus certifies the absolute energy tolerance τ whenever ρ/√μ ≤ τ. This check works even when the quality of the updated hierarchy is unknown. A pass certifies the requested tolerance. A failure can cost extra iterations even when the answer is already accurate enough. How much are we paying for that margin? <a class="citation" id="cite-21-5" href="#ref-21" aria-label="Source 21">[21]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-52-4" href="#ref-52" aria-label="Source 52">[52]</a></p><p>For a nonzero residual, call the ratio of the exact-residual bound to the true energy error R. On an eigenmode with eigenvalue λ, R = √(λ/μ). For the four-cycle’s missed mode this is exactly √3, at any contrast. Higher-energy modes can produce much larger overestimates. Rounding control adds a separate factor ρ/||r||₂. On known solutions we can measure the iterations each margin costs.</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="R^2 = (r^T r)/(mu r^T A&#x27;^(-1) r),     1 &lt;= R &lt;= sqrt(lambda_max(A&#x27;)/mu)."><i>R</i><sup>2</sup> = (<i>r</i><sup>T</sup><i>r</i>)/(μ <i>r</i><sup>T</sup><i>A</i>′<sup>-1</sup><i>r</i>),<br/>1 ≤ <i>R</i> ≤ √(λ<sub>max</sub>(<i>A</i>′)/μ).</div><p>The check must apply to the calculation that actually ran. In pipelined conjugate gradients the stored residual can look small while the residual of the returned iterate is still large. Cools and collaborators supplied a rule for selectively recomputing it. In a finite-element benchmark a joint NaN mask can drop a required location precisely when the prediction there is missing. Those two failures call for different repairs. Recompute against the original equations in the first case. Establish the required domain independently and check coverage in the second. Both repairs can be automated once the error has been identified. <a class="citation" id="cite-33-1" href="#ref-33" aria-label="Source 33">[33]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-95-1" href="#ref-95" aria-label="Source 95">[95]</a></p><p>Even an exact low-rank approximation can expose a false solver guarantee. In Huh, Cartis and Nakatsukasa’s APLICUR analysis, an allowed target of one half with regularization one gives a two-coordinate system with condition number 2 after preconditioning. Theorem 4.1 bounds it by √5/2. The restriction matters one step later. On a second two-coordinate system, one LSQR step leaves about 0.579 of the initial projected residual norm. Lemma 4.2 promises at most 0.2295. The proof used the regularization as a lower bound after rescaling a retained direction below it. The regularized Algorithm 2 already chooses a target at or above that level. <a href="#aplicur-printed-bounds">Appendix B</a> gives both calculations and the correction. <a class="citation" id="cite-87-1" href="#ref-87" aria-label="Source 87">[87]</a></p><p>Recomputing can repair a bad residual. A decisive test can also force you to abandon a successful formulation. Who is most likely to ask for such a test, and when does their advice repay the cost of getting it?</p></section><section id="which-work-remains-scarce"><h2>8. What did the second investigator contribute?</h2><p>In the Grothendieck-constant project the decisive human act was to stop a search that was still producing mathematics. Six variations on a rounding scheme had failed. The operators asked for a theorem limiting the entire class. The possibility of reversing the search was already in the brief. The agent found the Naor-Regev result that could turn a sufficiently general ceiling on the schemes into a lower bound on the constant. Fourteen later sessions proved restricted sublemmas while the bound for the whole class stayed out of reach. The operators redirected the effort again. <a class="citation" id="cite-105-2" href="#ref-105" aria-label="Source 105">[105]</a></p><p>This time the agent found the reason the earlier arguments would not extend. The quantities they controlled did not average properly when schemes were mixed. Two Hermite coefficients did. An affine inequality in those coefficients survives taking mixtures. The operators pushed for a member of the new family that could actually be proved. One complete result was the lower bound 6π/11. The human recognized that another local theorem would leave the main question open. The machine invented the mathematical relation that survived the passage to the full class. <a class="citation" id="cite-105-3" href="#ref-105" aria-label="Source 105">[105]</a> On Erdős’s minimum-overlap problem the investigators gave the Station agents the opposite brief. The problem asks how small the largest overlap over translations can be for a balanced partition, using the continuum version in which the two parts have complementary densities and equal mass. The supplied brief was to improve the upper bound. The agents coupled two Fourier measurements of that overlap and proved a lower bound above 0.380552. They chose to turn the inequality around and delivered a certificate for the direction they chose. <a class="citation" id="cite-106-1" href="#ref-106" aria-label="Source 106">[106]</a></p><p>Archon measures the effect of advice at a proof checkpoint. Its planning and Lean agents had three obligations left in a proof of unique factorization. A supervisor supplied a Krull-domain blueprint. The system kept its own Kaplansky strategy, borrowed intermediate observations and finished in 2 hours 12 minutes. Without the blueprint the same system finished in 3 hours 43 minutes. The advised run finished 91 minutes sooner. The clock started after the blueprint was written. A fair comparison would start it earlier and give a second agent a turn at writing the advice. <a class="citation" id="cite-90-1" href="#ref-90" aria-label="Source 90">[90]</a></p><p>TasteVal put a human or a model in charge of the same coding agent. Each chose the next experiment after seeing what the last one bought. The strongest tested model, Opus 5.5 at maximum effort, declined one of the eight machine-learning tasks. Across the other seven it had an estimated 2.25-fold advantage in experimental compute efficiency against the best human attempt per task. The 95 percent confidence interval ran from 1.16 to 4.29. Here two machines could beat an expert directing the same instrument. The investigators had supplied the score. The next comparison has to give both teams room to decide what is worth measuring. <a class="citation" id="cite-159-2" href="#ref-159" aria-label="Source 159">[159]</a></p><p>The operators in the Grothendieck project saw fourteen sessions that failed to settle the global bound. A second agent deserves that history, an independent notebook and the chance to stop the search too. This is what I would test. When the same cheap evidence supports several explanations that would send the work in different directions, does a specialist find the consequential distinction sooner, and does the result repay the cost of finding it? <a class="citation" id="cite-105-4" href="#ref-105" aria-label="Source 105">[105]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-124-1" href="#ref-124" aria-label="Source 124">[124]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-128-1" href="#ref-128" aria-label="Source 128">[128]</a></p></section><section id="automation-in-numerical-research"><h2>9. Who knows when to change the calculation?</h2><p>Give both teams the relation to test and you can compare how well they check it. Start one step earlier. Who sees a reason to make that test in the first place? Nguyen’s cosmology study records two distinct occasions to change a calculation. In the first, the errors were already visible. Across 33 sessions a coding agent tuned an analytic projection while errors remained 8-86 percent. It had found an alternative path in the code and defended the path it was using. The physicist identified the missing directional dependence of baryon-acoustic-oscillation damping and proposed angular quadrature for the projection. The agent implemented it in one session. Four of six affected outputs came within one percent of their references. <a class="citation" id="cite-101-2" href="#ref-101" aria-label="Source 101">[101]</a></p><p>The second occasion was harder to see. A fitted scalar brought the remaining discrepancies within the tests. The physicist asked which term belonged inside the integral. Restoring its angular dependence removed the fitted parameter. In the first episode expertise redirected a visibly failing search. In the second it explained why an apparent success still needed a different calculation. Parts of this account were reconstructed from memory. Nguyen did not replay either checkpoint with a fresh autonomous team. <a class="citation" id="cite-101-3" href="#ref-101" aria-label="Source 101">[101]</a></p><p>After42’s agents caught an erasure in their own work. They were studying how random edge costs change the geometry of a spanning tree. On a cycle each tree omits one edge. Average over independent, identically distributed costs and the missing edge is uniform at every temperature. The law of a single tree on the cycle cannot reveal the temperature dependence the agents wanted to study. So they held the costs fixed, sampled two trees independently and asked how far the distances between the same two vertices could differ. A person supplied the broad goal. The general conjecture is still open, but the comparison that made it worth pursuing in this form was the agents’ idea. <a class="citation" id="cite-134-1" href="#ref-134" aria-label="Source 134">[134]</a></p><p>In a spring simulation, multiplying the stiffness by 32 barely changed the deflection. All six runs hit the travel stop. The agent proposed changing the number of springs. The senior researcher asked how the load was shared and whether the stop had hidden the effect of stiffness. Give five models the project record and both proposed plans in either order, and all ten responses pick the senior researcher’s plan. Across 248 decisions selected from earlier human corrections, fresh researchers with AI available recognized the recorded judgment 73.4 percent of the time. The best tested model scored 63.5 percent. The larger study measures recognition of a supplied alternative. The senior researcher in the spring case did something harder by putting the travel stop on the list of explanations in the first place. Once that proposal was on the page, every tested model chose it. <a class="citation" id="cite-61-1" href="#ref-61" aria-label="Source 61">[61]</a></p><p>On a sign-uncertainty problem the agents found the turn themselves. Their evaluator accepted Laguerre polynomials with at most twenty prescribed positive double roots. An exact obstruction on 41 tail points showed that no construction in that family could bring the target below 0.315305. The agents retained the Laguerre basis, abandoned the prescribed roots and certified 0.3089 with a polynomial the evaluator could not score. The machines had turned failure into a theorem about the search itself. They found exactly which assumption to abandon. Anyone assigning that act to the human by definition has already lost the argument. <a class="citation" id="cite-106-2" href="#ref-106" aria-label="Source 106">[106]</a></p><p>A machine can also invent the missing experiment. In a virtual lab the brief asked for a material’s conductivity at room temperature. In the virtual experiment, strong heating made the material more conductive. The simulator used to fit the data assumed constant conductivity. One agent pooled its hot runs and missed the answer. Astra tried gentle heating and recovered the room-temperature value. Astra invented the gentler experiment. The brief had already settled which temperature mattered. <a class="citation" id="cite-160-1" href="#ref-160" aria-label="Source 160">[160]</a></p><p>Given a curvature-based starting point for Allen-Cahn, agents found an ansatz that coupled two shape modes. It predicted that doubling the initial elongation would roughly quadruple the amplitude of the fourth harmonic, as separate simulations confirmed. <a class="citation" id="cite-169-1" href="#ref-169" aria-label="Source 169">[169]</a> In turbulent channel flow, agent-designed wall models improved the prescribed score, while the conventional model still matched the reference velocity profile more closely in the logarithmic layer. <a class="citation" id="cite-170-1" href="#ref-170" aria-label="Source 170">[170]</a></p><p>What if two scientists want different things from exactly the same measurements? Isaacson and colleagues took the same electrode readings from a tank containing two agar lungs and an agar heart. The D-bar reconstruction separated the lungs and recovered about 93 percent of the known heart-to-lung conductivity range. The NOSER reconstruction recovered about 27 percent, yet the authors judged the lungs’ overall size and position more faithful in its image. An investigator who asks whether the lungs can be distinguished and one who asks how large they are have reasons to trust different features of the very same measurements. A numerical guarantee has to say which question it answers. <a class="citation" id="cite-161-1" href="#ref-161" aria-label="Source 161">[161]</a></p><p>Let both teams question the receiving scientist. On matched cases give them the same written brief instead, with the regions, precision and storage requirements already specified. Give me a catalogue of allowed measurements and a cheap, reliable way to score each design, and I would try a second agent here too. A written brief tells both teams what they must guarantee. Finding a test that earns the guarantee can be the discovery. Charge the interviews, rejected proposals and proof to the teams. If the gain concentrates in the interview cases, trace what each team learned from the scientist and how it turned that into a measurement. If it persists with the requirements on paper, look at the mathematical design. The brief and the independent checker must be the same for both teams within each comparison. Give the best machine-only procedure the same total budget. This comparison is still to be run. <a href="#comparing-investigators-on-the-mesh-and-sumset-problems">Appendix C</a></p><p>If the agents find the consequential missing observations as early and as cheaply as the partnership, I would give them the next mesh. In Kasparov’s freestyle chess both sides knew the object of the game. On an open brief either team can discover what is worth playing for. Let each choose a successor problem, then exchange their proposals anonymously and have the other team pursue them. Give each team a chance to pursue its own question as well. A fruitful question can improve even a rival’s mathematics. <a class="citation" id="cite-89-1" href="#ref-89" aria-label="Source 89">[89]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-90-2" href="#ref-90" aria-label="Source 90">[90]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-105-5" href="#ref-105" aria-label="Source 105">[105]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-123-1" href="#ref-123" aria-label="Source 123">[123]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-124-2" href="#ref-124" aria-label="Source 124">[124]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-132-2" href="#ref-132" aria-label="Source 132">[132]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-133-2" href="#ref-133" aria-label="Source 133">[133]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-134-2" href="#ref-134" aria-label="Source 134">[134]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-135-1" href="#ref-135" aria-label="Source 135">[135]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-137-1" href="#ref-137" aria-label="Source 137">[137]</a></p><p>See also <a href="https://refactor.press/essays/what-sustains-mathematical-expertise#sustaining-independent-capability">What Sustains Mathematical Expertise</a>.</p></section><section id="where-to-concentrate"><h2>10. What could last after the discovery is public</h2><p>The sharpest test is a theorem close enough to its predecessor to tempt you into copying the proof. In the Schiffer problem the outward derivative of an eigenfunction vanished on the boundary. The proof could discard certain harmonic coefficients. In the Berenstein problem that derivative was a nonzero constant. Those same coefficients now carried information needed to enforce the boundary condition. Colbrook, Sadeghi and Stepaniants found a candidate shape by Fourier search, moved to a fixed disk, kept the coefficients and bounded the omitted modes. They could certify a noncircular domain. <a class="citation" id="cite-107-1" href="#ref-107" aria-label="Source 107">[107]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-108-1" href="#ref-108" aria-label="Source 108">[108]</a></p><p>The researchers gave ChatGPT the earlier manuscript and its working code. The predecessor already contained the central construction, the fixed-disk formulation and the strategy for controlling the tail. The new paper credits ChatGPT with ideas for early versions of two proof steps and changes to the code. The authors developed and independently checked them. The old proof told them which coefficients they could throw away. The changed boundary condition made keeping those coefficients the decisive move. <a class="citation" id="cite-107-2" href="#ref-107" aria-label="Source 107">[107]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-108-2" href="#ref-108" aria-label="Source 108">[108]</a></p><p>Start the clock before either team chooses its question, and charge my hours as well as the agents’. On the next mesh I would ask the scientist who inherited the old runs which conclusion they could no longer draw. Work backward from that loss. Exhibit two fields the old measurements call identical. Find an observation that separates those fields, then prove that the saved record recovers every required patch mass within the scientist’s tolerance. Publish that observation, its error bound and the code that measures it. Publish the cofactor recurrences and their certificate too. The two agents get to start the next problem with everything the mathematicians just taught them. Any lasting advantage has to survive that gift. <a class="citation" id="cite-105-6" href="#ref-105" aria-label="Source 105">[105]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-130-2" href="#ref-130" aria-label="Source 130">[130]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-133-3" href="#ref-133" aria-label="Source 133">[133]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-134-3" href="#ref-134" aria-label="Source 134">[134]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-135-2" href="#ref-135" aria-label="Source 135">[135]</a> <span id="cite-145-5" aria-hidden="true"></span><a class="citation" id="cite-145-3" href="#ref-145" aria-label="Source 145">[145]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-148-2" href="#ref-148" aria-label="Source 148">[148]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-151-12" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-152-2" href="#ref-152" aria-label="Source 152">[152]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-163-4" href="#ref-163" aria-label="Source 163">[163]</a></p><p>A mathematician can invent a method so good that nobody needs its inventor on the next hundred cases. That is a mathematical success. When the hundred-and-first case breaks the method and the old checks still pass, who finds the missing distinction, and what does it cost?</p></section><section class="essay-notes" id="mathematical-and-benchmark-record"><h2>Appendix A. The mathematical and benchmark record</h2><h3>What the released mathematics catalogue establishes</h3><p>The OpenAI release examined on 4 November lists 721 manuscripts in 373 related families. The repository describes them as raw research data at different stages of verification. Three manuscripts were withdrawn after a shared sign error, and several others were repaired. Theoretical computer science has the most released families. Probability and statistical mechanics has the most manuscripts. The table uses the publisher’s primary subject assignments. <a class="citation" id="cite-4-2" href="#ref-4" aria-label="Source 4">[4]</a></p><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Released catalogue coverage at commit 4eaee875, dated 30 October 2026. A family can contain several related manuscripts. The final column counts families with links to Lean documentation for selected statements. <a class="citation" id="cite-4-3" href="#ref-4" aria-label="Source 4">[4]</a></caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Subject</th><th scope="col">Families</th><th scope="col">Manuscripts</th><th scope="col">Families with Lean notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Number theory</td><td>31</td><td>58</td><td>17</td></tr><tr><td>Algebraic and complex geometry</td><td>36</td><td>86</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Real and complex analysis</td><td>16</td><td>27</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>Convex and metric geometry</td><td>15</td><td>30</td><td>13</td></tr><tr><td>Theoretical computer science</td><td>41</td><td>74</td><td>35</td></tr><tr><td>Dynamical systems and ergodic theory</td><td>12</td><td>19</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Combinatorics</td><td>37</td><td>50</td><td>33</td></tr><tr><td>Algebra</td><td>18</td><td>29</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>Probability and statistical mechanics</td><td>29</td><td>105</td><td>19</td></tr><tr><td>Mathematical logic</td><td>6</td><td>8</td><td>6</td></tr><tr><td>Group theory</td><td>14</td><td>22</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Mathematical physics</td><td>25</td><td>59</td><td>17</td></tr><tr><td>Operator algebras</td><td>19</td><td>31</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>Topology</td><td>18</td><td>26</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Functional analysis</td><td>11</td><td>19</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>Differential geometry</td><td>29</td><td>49</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>Partial differential equations</td><td>16</td><td>29</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>373</td><td>721</td><td>245</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The release began from approximately 4,000 posed problems and applied aggregation and a significance filter. Attempted-problem totals by field are unavailable. The 245 family documentation links cover selected statements of varying scope. The repository’s approximately 42 percent figure counts formalized principal results using a different unit. Its classification has no numerical-analysis category. Identifying computational results requires examining individual papers. <a class="citation" id="cite-4-4" href="#ref-4" aria-label="Source 4">[4]</a> In the released arithmetic-progression reasoning record, Part II’s opening prompt specifies the successor target. Other projects grant broader discretion. Zheng and colleagues let a pipeline recover open conjectures from the combinatorics literature. It connected an existing finite-geometric family of tangent lines to a conjecture about their union, disproving a proposed route to a Nikodym bound. The people who formulated the conjecture, the agent that recovered and attacked it, and the researchers who checked the new connection did different work. <a class="citation" id="cite-4-5" href="#ref-4" aria-label="Source 4">[4]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-139-1" href="#ref-139" aria-label="Source 139">[139]</a></p><p>In block theory OpenAI reports proofs of the blockwise Alperin weight conjecture for every finite group and prime and of Donovan finiteness over each fixed algebraically closed field with bounded defect-group order. I have not located independent specialist validation of either whole proof. Separate formalization records cover the statement that rational Hodge classes on complex CM abelian varieties are algebraic in every dimension and codimension, and specified Auslander-Reiten and Tachikawa counterexamples. Each record applies to its own stated result. <a class="citation" id="cite-5-2" href="#ref-5" aria-label="Source 5">[5]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-6-2" href="#ref-6" aria-label="Source 6">[6]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-7-2" href="#ref-7" aria-label="Source 7">[7]</a></p><p>The Saxl manuscript provides a second structural comparison. For the staircase partition ρ = (m,m−1,…,1), it tracks the cyclic module generated by the tensor product of its chosen row and column vectors. Coordinate projections separate a smaller staircase from a band. The proof must retain the actual generator and identify the projected image with the full induced module through explicit inverse maps. Mere occurrence of the desired constituents somewhere in the ambient tensor square would not supply the induction. A width-two band becomes a path contraction of 2 × 2 matrices, and dual-polytabloid pairings detect the required constituents. <a class="citation" id="cite-99-2" href="#ref-99" aria-label="Source 99">[99]</a></p><p>Even the constituent test needs a repair. In Example 5.4 an invertible segment product B = 12T, with T exchanging the two coordinates, has B₁₁ = 0. The fixed endpoint pairing consequently vanishes. Conjugating the dual test basis by the matrix Q with rows (1,1) and (1,2) makes the conjugated product’s (1,1) entry equal to 12 while preserving the original generator and endpoint letters. The final proof explicitly handles an uninformative test by changing the representation used to inspect the object. It supplies no chronological record of the model first failing and then discovering this repair. The formalization documentation reports the Saxl and universal-square positivity results, while excluding the stronger prescribed-tensor orbit-span assertion. <a class="citation" id="cite-99-3" href="#ref-99" aria-label="Source 99">[99]</a></p><p>The exact Fourier-transform manuscript counts preparation and indexing but leaves floating-point stability and useful crossover unresolved. The matrix-multiplication family gives an arithmetic-exponent improvement without a competitive finite matrix size. Each leaves room for further mathematical work on finite costs. <a class="citation" id="cite-56-1" href="#ref-56" aria-label="Source 56">[56]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-100-1" href="#ref-100" aria-label="Source 100">[100]</a></p><h3>What the benchmarks and field reports measure</h3><p>First Proof’s thirty experts assessed anonymized solutions to ten research problems known to have solutions of at most twelve pages. Systems had 24 hours without human input during testing. Its rubric counts an average correctness score of at least 0.5, which allows minor repairable errors. The reported third-batch costs for GPT-6 Astra and ProofCouncil were $36 and $1,888 respectively, excluding benchmark creation and review. In the October organizers’ retrospective scoring, the OpenAI entry went from five credited answers to seven and ProofCouncil from seven to nine, with lower reported batch costs. ProofCouncil’s July system report describes six June submissions as correct up to minor revisions under the editorial judgments it reports. <a class="citation" id="cite-124-3" href="#ref-124" aria-label="Source 124">[124]</a> The organizers changed the problems and some testing conditions between batches. <a class="citation" id="cite-8-2" href="#ref-8" aria-label="Source 8">[8]</a></p><p>TheoPhysEval used an automated final-answer checker on 50 expert-contributed theoretical-physics calculations. Astra with Codex reached 49/50 with web access and 46/50 without web search or the supplied background papers. Both tracks retained computational tools. The authors say the answers were unpublished at evaluation, some contributors had used AI, and they could not exclude leakage. Their test ran each whole track once per agent. <a class="citation" id="cite-9-1" href="#ref-9" aria-label="Source 9">[9]</a></p><p>Das and Fox credit GPT-5.6 Sol with the Murty-Simon edge bound floor(n²/4) and its even-order equality case. The authors simplified the proof and established the equality cases for all orders. Oum expounds OpenAI’s cycle-double-cover proof. Nagda, Raghavan and Thakurta used AlphaEvolve to find combinatorial objects and reduction gadgets. Xu’s cubic counterexample to the general Bilu-Linial signing conjecture and Lin and Zhou’s revised spectral-signing work also report model contributions. The question for Ramanujan base graphs and efficient construction questions have their own hypotheses. <a class="citation" id="cite-11-1" href="#ref-11" aria-label="Source 11">[11]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-12-1" href="#ref-12" aria-label="Source 12">[12]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-13-1" href="#ref-13" aria-label="Source 13">[13]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-14-1" href="#ref-14" aria-label="Source 14">[14]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-15-1" href="#ref-15" aria-label="Source 15">[15]</a></p><p>OpenAI’s October local-smoothing release asserts a Euclidean wave estimate in every spatial dimension at least two, together with formalization records and related restriction and Kakeya statements. These remain claims of the releasing authors, whose mathematical scope requires independent assessment. The Darcy example in the main text has expert benchmark review. <a class="citation" id="cite-8-3" href="#ref-8" aria-label="Source 8">[8]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-16-3" href="#ref-16" aria-label="Source 16">[16]</a></p><h3>Further changes of representation and proof strategy</h3><p>Aletheia found another use for a change of representation. Tony Feng credits the agent with connecting arithmetic eigenweights to symmetric-group representation theory through algebraic combinatorics and determining them for all classical groups. In type D of even rank, a commutativity assumption needed for an earlier result can fail. Two natural generators have to be treated through their two-dimensional span. Apart from building the agent, Feng describes his own role as rewriting its mathematics into a paper. On independence polynomials the division was different. Gemini 2.5 Deep Think helped establish a key inequality. Aletheia later suggested dual sets and the broader strategy. The human authors proved some of its suggested statements independently. <span id="cite-3-3" aria-hidden="true"></span><a class="citation" id="cite-3-2" href="#ref-3" aria-label="Source 3">[3]</a></p><p>The Saxl example above gives a useful distinction. The proof must keep its generator fixed. What changes is the dual basis used to inspect a segment product. For the invertible product 12T, using that freedom changes the observed entry from zero to twelve. <a class="citation" id="cite-99-4" href="#ref-99" aria-label="Source 99">[99]</a></p><p>An agent can invent a method that leaves the specialist a different job. Alman and Vassilevska Williams credit Claude with an algorithm they then understood, simplified, strengthened and extended. For polynomially bounded integer inputs their deterministic bounds include O(n<sup>1.9992</sup>) for 3SUM and O(n<sup>2.99942</sup>) for directed all-pairs shortest paths with no negative cycles. The contribution is mathematical even though those exponents give no practical crossover. In operator theory Townsend and Greenbaum report that they and Crouzeix checked Jin’s AI-assisted proof of Crouzeix’s conjecture and believe it correct. Its decisive theorem emerged in an approximately sixteen-hour model run. Designing a numerical method that can use the theorem economically is a further research question, open to either partner. <a class="citation" id="cite-17-1" href="#ref-17" aria-label="Source 17">[17]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-18-1" href="#ref-18" aria-label="Source 18">[18]</a></p><p>Two model-produced graph manuscripts already combine compression with a bill for recovering what survives. The strong-thin-tree construction controls cut loads while retaining the rank information needed to extract a spanning tree. Its signing routine requires rational vectors with covariance exactly equal to the identity. The proof approximates the frame rationally, adds vectors that exactly complete the covariance, checks positive semidefiniteness and transfers the estimate back after removing the added vectors. Its claimed C/k cut bound has polynomial bit complexity. With the displayed constant 2²⁰ and error 2²⁰ <i>r</i><sup>−1/8</sup>, the coefficient in the next packing bound turns positive only for r &gt; 2¹⁶⁸. Even positivity need not retain one whole tree. The proof absorbs smaller cases into an absolute constant. The calculation shows how conservative its sufficient guarantee is. A practical crossover is a separate question, as is two-sided spectral approximation. <a class="citation" id="cite-97-1" href="#ref-97" aria-label="Source 97">[97]</a></p><p>The matching construction changes sparse forests without losing the physical value of an edge. When it deletes a copy it transfers that copy’s accumulated increment to a base value. It records motion before cancellation so it can detect stale values and pay for refreshing them. A progress account pays for exploring local projections, while exact dyadic updates carry the precision requirement. Its claimed almost-linear randomized word-RAM bound charges allocation, multiword arithmetic, unsuccessful paths, scheduling and output. No competitive finite size follows from the stated asymptotic bound. An algorithm that recognizes stale values and budgets for their repair gives the next team a better starting point, whoever joins it. <a class="citation" id="cite-98-1" href="#ref-98" aria-label="Source 98">[98]</a></p><p>Structural mathematics already rewards this kind of economy. Gröbner-basis algorithms use signatures and syzygies to discard work. Roune and Stillman cut the potentially quadratic number of stored S-pair monomials to linear while retaining an integer per queued pair. In the Liquid Tensor Experiment, understanding what formalization required let Commelin and Scholze replace stable-homotopy machinery with a weaker sufficient result. Each discovery spared later users work. Every team that starts from the published result inherits that saving. <a class="citation" id="cite-93-1" href="#ref-93" aria-label="Source 93">[93]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-94-1" href="#ref-94" aria-label="Source 94">[94]</a></p><p>Bolzano records how a machine-proposed object and a human-proposed proof strategy fit together. Its prover agents invented a search problem in which finding a first collision determines where to look for a second. Four rounds produced a flawed proof by cases. Pavel Hubáček suggested asking what information the collisions in a proposed reduction actually reveal. The point was to prove that, with overwhelming probability, the reduction circuit contained a collision that revealed no solution to the nested problem. Four more rounds produced a proof the authors judged largely complete. The machines invented the object. The specialist saw how to prove a limit on what could be learned about it. The authors never replayed that checkpoint with a fresh machine strategist. <a class="citation" id="cite-125-1" href="#ref-125" aria-label="Source 125">[125]</a></p><h3>Additional cases of mathematical invention</h3><p>In Das and Fox’s proof of the Murty-Simon conjecture, OpenAI’s GPT-5.6 Sol supplied the edge bound and the even-order equality case. The authors simplified the argument and established which graphs attain equality at every order. A person can edit a proof, extend it, explain it or launch another investigation from it. Those are different contributions. <a class="citation" id="cite-11-2" href="#ref-11" aria-label="Source 11">[11]</a></p><p>Blind review offers a different check. First Proof gave each system twenty-four hours to tackle ten problems without human help. GPT-6 Astra earned credit on seven and the independently developed ProofCouncil on nine. On the Darcy inverse problem all three reviewers judged ProofCouncil’s mathematics essentially correct. In TheoPhysEval, some apparent Astra errors sent the experts back to their own reference solutions. They found mistakes there and corrected them. The Darcy reviewers wanted Richter’s inversion formula credited where the proof used it. Contributing a better bound and explaining where its ingredients came from are two jobs, and the reviewers could tell which one the system had done. <a class="citation" id="cite-8-4" href="#ref-8" aria-label="Source 8">[8]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-9-2" href="#ref-9" aria-label="Source 9">[9]</a></p><p>In Part II of the arithmetic-progression reasoning record a human prompt names the quasipolynomial strengthening. The model identifies where the proof lost precision and devises the new move. The target and the proof have different origins. <a class="citation" id="cite-4-6" href="#ref-4" aria-label="Source 4">[4]</a></p><p>John Napier knew what he wanted logarithms to save. In his 1614 preface he described the time lost multiplying large numbers and the errors the work invited. He offered different numbers on which simpler operations would do the job. Michael Atiyah took that familiar motive much further in 1984. Because computers excel at long calculations, he wrote, “they will not search for alternatives.” He had already observed in the same essay that computer time makes faster algorithms valuable. Napier explained why he wanted a shortcut. Atiyah took the motive for a qualification its inventor would need. A machine need not find arithmetic tedious to discover a way around it. <a class="citation" id="cite-112-1" href="#ref-112" aria-label="Source 112">[112]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-81-1" href="#ref-81" aria-label="Source 81">[81]</a></p><p>Once a research move can be written down, it can travel without its author. Shanmu Jin adapted the procedure made public for the cycle-double-cover problem to ask for a proof of Crouzeix’s conjecture. Different machine branches attacked one another’s proposals. The decisive theorem emerged during a run of roughly sixteen hours. Townsend, Greenbaum and Crouzeix examined the proof and reported that they believe it correct. Jin chose the target and organized the search. The model found the theorem. Any team that inherits the procedure begins with more than its own ingenuity. <a class="citation" id="cite-12-2" href="#ref-12" aria-label="Source 12">[12]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-17-2" href="#ref-17" aria-label="Source 17">[17]</a></p><p>Zeilberger’s ambition already has descendants who set problems for people. Asked about graph invariants, TxGraffiti conjectured that in a connected cubic graph the independence number is at most three halves of the total domination number. Caro, Davila, Henning and Pepper proved the inequality for every regular degree at least two with the factor changed to half the degree, and characterized equality. FunSearch produced a constructor with independent cyclic symmetries. Its researchers recognized them and restricted the next search to symmetric admissible sets. One system gave people a theorem to generalize. The other gave them a representation in which to pose a new search. Neither project gave a second machine the same opening. <a class="citation" id="cite-83-1" href="#ref-83" aria-label="Source 83">[83]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-117-2" href="#ref-117" aria-label="Source 117">[117]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-138-1" href="#ref-138" aria-label="Source 138">[138]</a></p><p>The machine is already a rival on Brualdi’s ground. OpenAI constructed a fixed 66-by-66 zero-one pattern and large matrices in which a uniform sample of rows and columns is a poor guide to how much work it takes to remove the pattern. A copy must preserve the two orders and match every zero and one. Along the constructed family, the copies become rarer than any power of the fraction of entries that must be changed to remove them all. An investigator who trusts the sampled counts can overlook an expensive defect. A machine built the matrices that expose it. I would pursue this kind of combinatorics because a good construction tells us exactly where a test fails. <a class="citation" id="cite-165-1" href="#ref-165" aria-label="Source 165">[165]</a></p><p>In the following Zeilberger example the software searches for a counting scheme. Success gives a recurrence that can be proved, as with the cofactor certificate in the body. <a class="citation" id="cite-82-2" href="#ref-82" aria-label="Source 82">[82]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-117-3" href="#ref-117" aria-label="Source 117">[117]</a></p><p>Wilf and Zeilberger had shown how a suitable hypergeometric sum could be proved by finding an identity that telescopes. Check the boundary terms and an initial value, and one certificate settles the sum for every admissible index. Zeilberger’s later program WILF searched for the counting scheme itself. Give it permutations that avoid the patterns 123 and 132, and allow it to inspect prefixes of length two. The count it yields is easy to see once you have the rule. The next entry must be one of the two largest remaining. Two later entries larger than it would make a forbidden triple whichever order they came in. For a permutation of length n there are two choices at each of the first n − 1 positions and one at the last. That counts the permutations at every size. <a class="citation" id="cite-82-3" href="#ref-82" aria-label="Source 82">[82]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-117-4" href="#ref-117" aria-label="Source 117">[117]</a></p><p>A structural experiment can be cheap, have an honest stopping point and pay in exact answers at every size. In Theorems for a Price Zeilberger also asked what one would pay for a proof after an experiment had already supplied strong evidence. To see what a live collaborator still contributes, give the agent the program and change the problem. <a class="citation" id="cite-117-5" href="#ref-117" aria-label="Source 117">[117]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-121-2" href="#ref-121" aria-label="Source 121">[121]</a></p></section><section class="essay-notes" id="solver-evidence-and-extensions"><h2>Appendix B. Solver evidence and extensions</h2><h3>Spectral comparison and approximate elimination</h3><p>A constant vector has zero energy. For a connected graph this is the entire kernel. One can fix a vertex potential or work on the mean-zero subspace to obtain a positive-definite problem. A related class consists of symmetric diagonally dominant matrices with nonpositive off-diagonal entries, abbreviated SDDM here. Such a matrix has the form A = L + Γ, where Γ is nonnegative diagonal. It is positive definite when every connected component has a positive grounding term. The scope here is this concrete matrix class. <a class="citation" id="cite-19-4" href="#ref-19" aria-label="Source 19">[19]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-26-2" href="#ref-26" aria-label="Source 26">[26]</a></p><p>A preconditioned iteration repeatedly applies an inexpensive approximation to the inverse, aiming to reduce the total work needed to solve the original system. Write that action as solving with an auxiliary matrix M. For symmetric positive-definite A and M, suppose positive constants α and β satisfy the following inequalities for every vector x.</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="alpha x^T M x &lt;= x^T A x &lt;= beta x^T M x.">α <i>x</i><sup>T</sup><i>Mx</i> ≤ <i>x</i><sup>T</sup><i>Ax</i> ≤ β <i>x</i><sup>T</sup><i>Mx</i>.</div><p>Then the generalized eigenvalues of A relative to M lie between α and β, and the corresponding condition number is at most β/α. For a fixed positive-definite preconditioner in exact arithmetic, this ratio controls the standard worst-case convergence bound for conjugate gradients. A smaller condition number alone need not produce fewer iterations on a particular problem, since the spectral distribution matters. The computational benefit depends on convergence together with the cost of constructing and applying M. For Laplacians the comparison is made on the orthogonal complement of their common kernel. <a class="citation" id="cite-52-5" href="#ref-52" aria-label="Source 52">[52]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-75-1" href="#ref-75" aria-label="Source 75">[75]</a></p><p>Multigrid approaches the loss of information in another way. A cheap relaxation reduces some error components, and a coarse correction represents those that remain. Algebraically smooth error is error that the chosen relaxation struggles to remove. Its meaning depends on the operator and relaxation. Strongly connected regions joined by weak edges can move almost independently at low energy. A coarse representation must account for the resulting modes. The relevant objects are vectors, matrix blocks, graph partitions, and transfer operators. <a class="citation" id="cite-20-3" href="#ref-20" aria-label="Source 20">[20]</a></p><p>Refine the mesh in the <a href="https://refactor.press/essays/continuity-as-organized-discrete-data#making-the-error-affordable-to-measure">Poisson calculation</a> and the zero function earns a smaller absolute Euclidean residual against the nodal test functions. Its relative residual stays at one. Its energy error against the continuum solution never improves. An agent rewarded for shrinking the absolute residual could beat its previous score every time without changing its answer. The missing ingredient is a bound connecting that particular measurement to the error we care about.</p><h3>What one elimination leaves for the next</h3><p>Gao, Kyng, and Spielman’s AC(2) samples interactions among the neighbors of an eliminated vertex and keeps the sample connected. It performs well on many symmetric diagonally dominant systems with nonpositive off-diagonal entries. The authors conjecture a relative condition number of O(log<sup>2</sup> m), possibly O(log m), with high probability for m input edges. The missing argument must control the random distortions as the Schur complement itself changes. <a class="citation" id="cite-26-3" href="#ref-26" aria-label="Source 26">[26]</a></p><p>A triangle shows why connectedness and the right average are insufficient. Start with unit weights. Choose one edge uniformly, give it weight 3 − 2ε, and give the other two weight ε, with 0 &lt; ε &lt; 1. Every outcome is connected and every edge still has expected weight one.</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="E[L_s] = L,     but kappa(L_s relative to L on the mean-zero subspace) = (2 - epsilon)/epsilon.">E[<i>L</i><sub>s</sub>] = <i>L</i>,<br/>κ(<i>L</i><sub>s</sub> relative to <i>L</i> on the mean-zero subspace) = (2 - ε)/ε.</div><p>If edge 12 is heavy, (1,1,−2) and (1,−1,0) span the mean-zero subspace and have relative eigenvalues ε and 2 − ε. Every realization can therefore be a poor approximation although the average Laplacian is exact. This calculation isolates the gap between those two properties and a spectral guarantee. Actual elimination rules such as VAC and CAST use additional structure. <a class="citation" id="cite-47-1" href="#ref-47" aria-label="Source 47">[47]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-48-2" href="#ref-48" aria-label="Source 48">[48]</a></p><p>CAST lets us follow one such choice into the calculation. Increasing its sampling parameter tightens a bound on the local second moment. It also introduces edges that later eliminations may have to process. Extra edges stay cheap on some low-degree graphs, propagate expensively on Chimera systems and have less new fill to create inside already dense blocks. Pivot degree alone cannot price the choice. At the base setting, CAST-1 retained AC’s ordering, factor representation, and solver path. Over 250 right-hand sides its mean per-system speedup over AC was 1.17 on 17 SuiteSparse matrices and 1.18 on 128 Chimera systems. <a class="citation" id="cite-48-3" href="#ref-48" aria-label="Source 48">[48]</a></p><p>The five Chimera graphs expose two different ways to make a bad choice. On graph i₃, CAST-1 was 1.38 times faster at four right-hand sides per factor. At 250, CAST-2 was 1.17 times faster. The crossover lay between ten and fifty solves. The matrices came from Newton steps. The right-hand sides were synthetic Gaussian draws projected to mean zero. The reported times include setup and solves to a verified relative residual of 10⁻⁸. On the other four graphs, CAST-2 was still 1.3 to 4.6 times slower at 250. Knowing the number of solves can reverse the decision. Knowing that number alone cannot settle it. The authors could inspect the elimination profile after building a first factor. How cheaply could we detect the costly fill before paying for it? <a class="citation" id="cite-48-4" href="#ref-48" aria-label="Source 48">[48]</a></p><p>The four-cycle tells us exactly what a two-level correction must do. A recursive hierarchy needs its own analysis. An extra spatial dimension can also reopen a settled choice. A learned correction close to the exact coarse solve on a two-dimensional test needed appreciably more iterations than the exact solve on the finest three-dimensional grid. The comparison appears below under learning a component of a solver, alongside other methods for constructing and reusing coarse directions. <a class="citation" id="cite-54-2" href="#ref-54" aria-label="Source 54">[54]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-78-3" href="#ref-78" aria-label="Source 78">[78]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-79-2" href="#ref-79" aria-label="Source 79">[79]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-85-1" href="#ref-85" aria-label="Source 85">[85]</a></p><h3 id="aplicur-printed-bounds">What APLICUR’s printed bounds require</h3><p>A small calculation can also locate a missing assumption in a proposed guarantee. APLICUR updates a low-rank preconditioner during an LSQR solve. In the authors’ Theorem 4.1 take the matrix and its rank-one CUR approximation both equal to diag(1,0), regularization μ = 1 and target t = 1/2. The preconditioned augmented matrix has singular values 1/2 and 1. The printed lower bound is 1, and its condition-number bound is √5/2. The actual condition number is 2. The approximation error here is zero. <a class="citation" id="cite-87-2" href="#ref-87" aria-label="Source 87">[87]</a></p><p>The point to repair is the retained block of the ideal Gram matrix. Its eigenvalue is t², while the omitted directions have eigenvalue μ². If μ ≤ t ≤ √(σ̂ℓ² + μ²), with σ̂ℓ the smallest retained singular value, and the CUR error E satisfies ||E||₂ &lt; μ, the preconditioned singular values lie between μ − ||E||₂ and t + ||E||₂. The regularized default satisfies the added target restriction. Each phase still needs the approximation-error condition. Note 87 gives the calculation from the paper’s preconditioner. <a class="citation" id="cite-87-3" href="#ref-87" aria-label="Source 87">[87]</a></p><p>Here is a separate witness for the convergence bound in Lemma 4.2 of v1. Take A = diag(1, 1/16), select its first row and column to obtain the rank-one CUR approximation Â = diag(1, 0), and set μ = 1, t = 1/2 and b = (2, 16). The residual of the approximation has norm 1/16. The target satisfies the printed upper restriction t² ≤ σ̂ℓ² + μ². For a single phase starting at zero, write M for the augmented matrix [A; I] right-preconditioned by diag(1/(2√2), 1). Its squared singular values are 1/4 and 257/256. In the corresponding two left singular directions, the augmented right-hand side (2, 16, 0, 0) has squared components 2 and 256/257. The column space of the augmented matrix is preserved by the right preconditioner, so these directions give the projected residual norm used in the lemma. <a class="citation" id="cite-87-4" href="#ref-87" aria-label="Source 87">[87]</a></p><p>After one LSQR step from zero, the residual, up to sign, is the augmented right-hand side minus α times its image under M <i>M</i><sup>T</sup>. Minimizing its norm gives α = (1/2 + 1)/(1/8 + 257/256) = 384/289. The multipliers on the two projected components are 193/289 and −193/578. The projected residual norm is therefore 193/√111265 times its initial value, about 0.5786. The right-hand factor in Lemma 4.2 for one phase and one step is 2(1 − (15/8)/(1 + √5/2)), about 0.2295. The case is permitted by the printed lemma. Algorithm 2 uses t = √(σ̂ℓ² + μ²) in the regularized SVD-based preconditioner, which excludes this witness. <a class="citation" id="cite-87-5" href="#ref-87" aria-label="Source 87">[87]</a></p><p>With the corrected singular-value bounds and e = ||E||₂, the Chebyshev factor for a phase is at most (t − μ + 2e)/(t + μ). This uses μ ≤ t ≤ √(σ̂ℓ² + μ²) and e &lt; μ in that phase. The phase bounds compose with the usual factor of two per phase and the corresponding power of each phase factor. The restriction and approximation-error hypothesis must hold each time a new preconditioner is used. <a class="citation" id="cite-87-6" href="#ref-87" aria-label="Source 87">[87]</a></p><h3>When checking dominates the cost</h3><p>Saving a quarter of the iterations can buy almost nothing in wall time. On 1,000 AirfRANS systems the selected learned smoother of Chillón and collaborators converged on 99.9 percent. It used about 27 percent fewer V-cycles than relaxed Jacobi but saved only about 4 percent of solve time. The network predicted coefficients during setup. Applying the resulting sparse smoother cost more per cycle than diagonal Jacobi. AirfRANS ran in double precision because single precision stalled. On separate fine three-dimensional canonical-flow tests in single precision, convergence averaged 67.7 percent across ten trained models. The tolerance was 10<sup>-4</sup> and the cap was 500 cycles. Both the workload and the way a model was selected belong beside its performance figure. <a class="citation" id="cite-40-1" href="#ref-40" aria-label="Source 40">[40]</a></p><p>Shui and colleagues found lower mean latency for selected human-assisted GPU kernels than for autonomous artifacts on five matched B200 operator definitions, by factors of 1.35 to 13.25. The fastest top-k indexer passed a three-trial check and then failed three of 200 targeted trials. An earlier fallback passed 1,206 targeted trials. The other four definitions retained reported latency advantages. Different model choices, development budgets and supplied context leave the contribution of human guidance unresolved. <a class="citation" id="cite-102-1" href="#ref-102" aria-label="Source 102">[102]</a></p><p>For N comparable queries, include setup and the cost of diagnosing, solving and checking each one. Recovery includes the further attempts and fallback allowed by the declared timeout rules. Report remaining failures as well as expected time.</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="T(N) = T_setup + N (T_diagnose + T_solve + T_check + E[T_recovery])."><i>T</i>(<i>N</i>) = <i>T</i><sub>setup</sub> + <i>N</i> (<i>T</i><sub>diagnose</sub> + <i>T</i><sub>solve</sub> + <i>T</i><sub>check</sub> + E[<i>T</i><sub>recovery</sub>]).</div><p>With extra setup cost ΔS and net saving d &gt; 0 per query, expected break-even begins at N ≥ ΔS/d. Costs after a coefficient change can be correlated, so the mean needs an accompanying distribution. A diagnostic that takes several solves to evaluate can exhaust a four-solve job. A rule discovered once and reused on thousands of such jobs can still repay a long investigation. Each deployment pays for its own probes, checks and recovery. That distinction matters just as much for a proof. A criterion that excludes a family of covers can be cheap to apply forever. Its discovery is one research result. The next comparison starts with that result available to everybody. <a class="citation" id="cite-48-5" href="#ref-48" aria-label="Source 48">[48]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-62-1" href="#ref-62" aria-label="Source 62">[62]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-86-2" href="#ref-86" aria-label="Source 86">[86]</a></p><h3>Accumulated error and coarse-space construction</h3><p>Ballard, Demmel, Holtz and Schwartz prove communication lower bounds for specified dense and sparse linear-algebra algorithms under stated memory-access models. Reducing arithmetic can leave data movement expensive. A new representation may change the executed algorithm and thus the costs to which those bounds apply. <a class="citation" id="cite-96-1" href="#ref-96" aria-label="Source 96">[96]</a></p><p>Changed coefficients can move the difficult error to a new subspace, and reducing communication can amplify rounding error. A stalled iteration could reflect a missing coarse direction, an inaccurate inner solve or drift in the recursively computed residual. A fresh residual can reveal the last of these without constructing a second hierarchy. Established replacement rules already automate parts of that diagnosis. <a class="citation" id="cite-20-4" href="#ref-20" aria-label="Source 20">[20]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-63-1" href="#ref-63" aria-label="Source 63">[63]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-95-2" href="#ref-95" aria-label="Source 95">[95]</a></p><p>The Simons workshop report connects practical benchmarks with theoretical solver design. It asks for parameterized matrix families suitable for both practical evaluation and theoretical analysis, together with algorithms that are fast in both settings. Its multigrid problem combines an implicit inverse with uniform spectral control, near-linear construction, and linear application cost for a polynomially bounded subclass of the grounded SDDM matrices defined at the start of this appendix. Its nonnormal-solver questions seek bounds describing the early behaviour of GMRES. These targets require the guarantee, its construction cost, and its predictive value to be considered together. <a class="citation" id="cite-75-2" href="#ref-75" aria-label="Source 75">[75]</a></p><p>Related algorithms show how local accuracy can control a complete solve. Kyng, Lee, Peng, Sachdeva, and Spielman analyze Schur-complement chains in which the global spectral distortion is bounded by twice the sum of the local error tolerances, in their exponential comparison convention, while application work is charged across the levels. The chain must control both the approximate Schur complements and the inverses used for eliminated blocks. The result applies to a different construction under different assumptions. It makes error allocation precise by relating local tolerances to a global accuracy budget and the work charged across levels. <a class="citation" id="cite-58-1" href="#ref-58" aria-label="Source 58">[58]</a></p><p>VAC replaces an elimination clique by a sampled spanning tree with the target marginals, connected support, and efficient sequential and parallel construction. CAST adds a local minimax characterization and a tunable bound on the normalized local second moment. A full-factorization spectral bound would also need to control the way local errors accumulate. <a class="citation" id="cite-47-2" href="#ref-47" aria-label="Source 47">[47]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-48-6" href="#ref-48" aria-label="Source 48">[48]</a></p><p>The literature supplies concrete constructions and limitations. Constrained local approximate ideal restriction combines local transfer construction with constraints on selected modes and uses aggressive coarsening to keep hierarchies economical. Optimal-transfer theory characterizes best choices for wider nonsymmetric and indefinite settings. Sparse and inexpensive realization requires additional work. Algebraic Schwarz methods construct coarse information from local generalized eigenproblems. In Al Daas, Jolivet, Nataf, and Tournier&#x27;s published analysis, the theorem concerns SPD matrices and its bound is not fully independent of the number of subdomains. Broader matrix classes appear in experiments. These distinctions identify the assumptions and costs a new construction would have to address. <a class="citation" id="cite-49-1" href="#ref-49" aria-label="Source 49">[49]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-50-1" href="#ref-50" aria-label="Source 50">[50]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-51-1" href="#ref-51" aria-label="Source 51">[51]</a></p><h3>Finding and certifying an unknown domain</h3><p>For the planar Berenstein conjecture the unknown is the domain itself. Can a bounded simply connected region other than a disk support a nonzero Laplace eigenfunction that vanishes on its boundary and has the same nonzero outward derivative everywhere there? Imposing both conditions makes the problem overdetermined. Colbrook, Sadeghi and Stepaniants searched for a shape using Fourier equations for boundary arclength. Those equations were good for finding a candidate and poor for bounding every omitted mode. To prove existence they pulled the problem back to a fixed disk and changed coordinates. Their estimates certify a noncircular domain with analytic boundary and a sign-changing eigenfunction. <a class="citation" id="cite-107-3" href="#ref-107" aria-label="Source 107">[107]</a></p><p>One detail explains why the earlier Schiffer proof could not simply be rerun. There the outward derivative vanished, which allowed certain harmonic coefficients to be eliminated. Berenstein’s nonzero derivative carried information in precisely those coefficients. The team kept them, coupled the interior and boundary equations, bounded the infinite tail, and recovered the required sign after squaring the boundary condition. ChatGPT received the predecessor manuscript and working code. The authors credit it with ideas for early versions of two proof steps and changes to the code, which they developed and checked. Reusing the framework meant discovering which part of it had ceased to be true. <a class="citation" id="cite-107-4" href="#ref-107" aria-label="Source 107">[107]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-108-3" href="#ref-108" aria-label="Source 108">[108]</a></p><p>There is a second route to the Schiffer result itself. Cao-Labora and de Dios Pont let the integer order of rotational symmetry vary as a real parameter. Most of those values describe an auxiliary problem. Uniform estimates let them follow bifurcating branches far enough to reach actual integers, yielding infinitely many noncircular domains. The first proof certifies an individual shape. The second creates room between symmetry orders in which a whole family becomes accessible. The authors of the computer-assisted Schiffer proof credit AI with debugging early code. In the second, models checked asymptotics numerically and drafted arguments for Bessel-function estimates. Even with the conjecture held fixed, the mathematical invention and the division of labor changed together. <a class="citation" id="cite-108-4" href="#ref-108" aria-label="Source 108">[108]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-111-1" href="#ref-111" aria-label="Source 111">[111]</a></p><h3>From a finite system to a PDE guarantee</h3><p>Differential equations supply important sparse matrices, but the initial solver problem can be specified by the matrix, the right-hand side, and the required error measure. Graph incidence and finite matrix inequalities are enough to state it. Sobolev spaces and homological language are not universal prerequisites for this programme. A claim of accuracy for a continuous PDE also needs a valid approximation argument. For smooth finite-difference problems this can use Taylor expansion and a discrete maximum principle. More general settings benefit from other tools. <a class="citation" id="cite-53-1" href="#ref-53" aria-label="Source 53">[53]</a></p><p>Compatible vector discretizations provide a demanding later application. Their edge and face incidences constrain the transfers, and their coefficient weights determine the energies. Shen and collaborators&#x27; three-dimensional H(curl) construction leaves a sparse multilevel realization open. Tuminaro and Glusa supply a different sparse construction with commuting constraints. The two papers vary coefficients in different terms, so one does not settle the other&#x27;s robustness question. Auxiliary-space and GenEO methods remain substantive alternatives. Arnold, Falk and Winther require a discrete subcomplex and uniformly bounded commuting projections for their mixed Hodge stability result. The <a href="#a-concrete-research-direction">Maxwell example in Section 6</a> illustrates the risk. <a class="citation" id="cite-22-3" href="#ref-22" aria-label="Source 22">[22]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-23-1" href="#ref-23" aria-label="Source 23">[23]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-24-1" href="#ref-24" aria-label="Source 24">[24]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-25-2" href="#ref-25" aria-label="Source 25">[25]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-43-3" href="#ref-43" aria-label="Source 43">[43]</a></p><p>A PDE application introduces an additional error between the continuous solution and the exact solution of the discrete system. To balance these two errors under mesh refinement, the solver needs a stopping tolerance suited to the current mesh. Certified reduced-basis methods and adaptive finite-element estimators address that further layer under explicit assumptions. Yano&#x27;s complementary-energy construction, for example, requires symmetric coercivity, affine parameter dependence, and specialized offline work. The p-robust adaptive analysis cited here has a computable contraction criterion and a stated Poisson setting. These are application-specific extensions, not prerequisites for studying the finite matrix algorithm. <a class="citation" id="cite-27-1" href="#ref-27" aria-label="Source 27">[27]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-46-1" href="#ref-46" aria-label="Source 46">[46]</a></p><p>Van den Berg and Breden get an effective global existence result for the one-dimensional Ohta-Kawasaki equation. Their coefficients approximate one trajectory from prescribed initial data. Bounds on the omitted modes validate successive intervals and a final step covering all future time. For the parameters and initial data in Theorem 1.2, the exact trajectory stays within 9 × 10⁻⁷ of the approximation, uniformly in space for every t ≥ 0. Staying in a narrow tube would still permit motion inside it forever. The final step also certifies a stable stationary solution and a neighborhood in which trajectories converge to it. That stability argument supplies the long-time conclusion. On a separate Swift-Hohenberg trajectory, their change of validation method made the proof about 25 times faster on the reported hardware. <a class="citation" id="cite-109-1" href="#ref-109" aria-label="Source 109">[109]</a></p><h3>Agent search and software optimization</h3><p>Coding, benchmarking, and revising a solver are already being automated. Bader and collaborators report agents improving the tuned VieCut minimum-cut implementation by factors of 1.28 sequentially and 1.63 at 32 threads on the real-world k-core workload. All 23 accepted edits received human review, and the same 53-instance workload was used for threshold tuning and reported timing. This is productive algorithm engineering under the stated conditions. <a class="citation" id="cite-28-1" href="#ref-28" aria-label="Source 28">[28]</a></p><p>FrontierCS 2 brings that competition into recent research code. Its 4 November 18-system, 48-task snapshot includes 13 systems passing Clusterwise SpGEMM, eight passing the KAMI numerical-kernel task, and seven passing Polar Express. Each task uses its own correctness and performance criteria. They are nevertheless actual successes in sparse and numerical algorithm engineering. <a class="citation" id="cite-69-1" href="#ref-69" aria-label="Source 69">[69]</a></p><p>ADSD provides a more targeted comparison. Across nine model/task comparisons, direct revision worsens error in five, whereas its combined diagnosis, literature-retrieval, and skill pipeline reports lower error throughout. Five rounds of direct revision on GOC-500 do not close the gap. This combined automated procedure can improve the tested searches beyond simply requesting another attempt. The ablation assesses diagnosis, retrieval, and skill construction together. Off-the-shelf solvers are excluded, and matching the final revision budget does not equalize the cost of constructing the pipeline. The result establishes a useful automated mechanism whose costs should be included in a comparison with specialist-led work. On the optimal-power-flow task the agent used the failure traces to ask how first-order quantities should be put on comparable scales. Its next diagnostic question arose inside the supplied optimization goal. <a class="citation" id="cite-30-1" href="#ref-30" aria-label="Source 30">[30]</a></p><p>AlphaEvolve can search over constructors and algorithms and use multiple evaluation scores, including expensive accelerator measurements. TTT-Discover updates model weights while working on the current problem, using feedback from earlier attempts. Its reported method targets one high-performing result under a continuous reward, with applications in mathematical bounds, GPU kernels, and algorithm engineering. Transfer of the learned procedure to further problems is a separate question. A comparison should include agents that learn during the investigation. <a class="citation" id="cite-44-1" href="#ref-44" aria-label="Source 44">[44]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-88-1" href="#ref-88" aria-label="Source 88">[88]</a></p><h3>Learning a component of a solver</h3><p>A research agent can discover a classical algorithm that uses no model when deployed. A neural component can predict a coarse space, a sparse correction, or a tuning decision. A surrogate can approximate a solution map. These are different mechanisms. For the finite programme, the central question is whether the resulting factor or hierarchy solves the original system at the required accuracy and total cost.</p><p>Sparse approximate inverses illustrate why representation design and hardware belong in the same argument. Yang and collaborators replace triangular applications with sparse matrix-vector products and train the admissible entries. Their comparisons change across matrix families and CPU/GPU implementations. Default multigrid remains strong on diffusion examples. The research decision concerns which representation preserves enough spectral information at the cost the hardware can actually deliver. <a class="citation" id="cite-77-1" href="#ref-77" aria-label="Source 77">[77]</a></p><p>Melchers, Dolean, and Abdelmalik compare four neural-operator coarse corrections while fixing the output coarse space. They vary input discretization and linear dependence on the right-hand side. Preserving right-hand-side linearity and matching the input projection to the output basis give the Neural Green&#x27;s Operator a Galerkin-type coarse correction. The experiments connect these choices to spectral behaviour, stagnation, and compatibility with Krylov solvers. Their November revision adds a three-dimensional diffusion test. On the finest grid, with 80 points per side, the learned correction took 37.3 flexible-GMRES iterations on average. The exact coarse solve took 23.0 with the same smoother, and the smoother alone took 124.1. The learned correction still helped substantially. Its near parity with the exact coarse solve on the two-dimensional test did not carry over. These counts tell us what to investigate before trusting the architecture on another grid. <a class="citation" id="cite-85-2" href="#ref-85" aria-label="Source 85">[85]</a></p><p>Bao, Cui, and Li learn coarse spaces from errors left behind by smoothing. Their objective compares subspaces independently of the basis used to express them. A perturbation analysis relates the discrepancy, including pre- and postsmoothing, to the resulting two-grid operator. The sharper quantity has a small Gram-matrix representation, but forming it requires solves with the original matrix, so it is used offline. The convergence bounds are conditional and the experiments concern stated parameterized families. The work exposes a concrete next problem of finding an informative, affordable test for when to reuse, enrich, or rebuild the learned space. <a class="citation" id="cite-86-3" href="#ref-86" aria-label="Source 86">[86]</a></p><p>RAPNet learns sparse corrections to coarse and transfer operators within an algebraic multigrid hierarchy, with transfer from training subgraphs of about 4,000 nodes to million-node tests. For some graph families the training patches come from large graphs to expose relevant structures. Its setup comparison uses classical CPU execution and GPU RAPNet, counting transfers and inference. A general convergence guarantee for unrestricted corrections remains open. These results demonstrate meaningful transfer, with its scope set by the training graphs, hardware, and stated limitations. <a class="citation" id="cite-37-1" href="#ref-37" aria-label="Source 37">[37]</a></p><p>AutoSpec learns short spectral recurrences, producing matrix polynomials for approximation, preconditioning, and filtering. Its comparisons include Chebyshev preconditioners supplied with the same spectral estimates and show both wins and losses. The tested operators are positive definite after the stated transformations. Some originally indefinite eigenproblems are squared, and some difficult linear systems also use incomplete Cholesky. Those details are part of what the results mean. Learning can thus return a compact executable numerical procedure with recognizable operations. An iteration-count improvement still needs a separate account of probe cost, training, and application time before becoming an end-to-end speedup. <a class="citation" id="cite-38-1" href="#ref-38" aria-label="Source 38">[38]</a></p><p>PCGBandit selects among 33 fixed preconditioner configurations during one OpenFOAM run and counts learning and backstop costs. In the Shercliff electric-potential solves, expensive early trials can absorb the savings of short runs. He and collaborators report total-runtime reductions of 36.1-43.3 percent on diffusion and 19.8-31.0 percent on diffusion-advection against the BoomerAMG reference default over 5,000-system sequences. In a separate same-hierarchy test on SPD diffusion, the mean first-attempt solve time favored a prescribed two-weight schedule by 2.8 percent, with a reported spread of 3.8 percent across six frozen learned states, whereas the same schedule failed frequently on variable-flow diffusion-advection. The strength of the comparator changes with the problem. <a class="citation" id="cite-39-1" href="#ref-39" aria-label="Source 39">[39]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-62-2" href="#ref-62" aria-label="Source 62">[62]</a></p><h3>Precision, storage and the cost of a solve</h3><p>Fast algebra and numerical stability can also be reconciled. Demmel, Dumitriu, and Holtz give normwise-stable algorithms for standard dense linear-algebra operations with essentially the matrix-multiplication exponent, under their stated arithmetic and stability assumptions. That result neither preserves arbitrary sparse structure nor establishes practical constants. A sparse implementation still needs control of its structure, accuracy, and application cost. <a class="citation" id="cite-60-1" href="#ref-60" aria-label="Source 60">[60]</a></p><p>The OpenAI release also reports constructions with explicit implementation-cost accounting. OpenAI&#x27;s manuscript on exact Fourier transforms reports an asymptotic operation bound below n log n while explicitly accounting for scalar preparation, schedule construction, and indexing. Its construction preserves working width and restores borrowed registers. The result assumes exact complex arithmetic, supplied roots of unity, and unrestricted coefficients. A floating-point realization would need its own stability analysis. The proposed architecture reaches well beyond a bare existence theorem. <a class="citation" id="cite-56-2" href="#ref-56" aria-label="Source 56">[56]</a> A separate OpenAI manuscript on integer multiplication explicitly propagates fixed-point errors and bounds the final coefficient error below one half so that rounding recovers the exact answer. Its asymptotic bit-complexity result comes with enormous constants, not a useful practical crossover. These are reported constructions, not independently certified here. These reports put finite-precision analysis among the tasks to include in a comparison with research agents. <a class="citation" id="cite-57-1" href="#ref-57" aria-label="Source 57">[57]</a></p><p>Arithmetic precision is another resource to allocate. McCormick, Benzaken, and Tamstorf develop a purely algebraic mixed-precision multigrid analysis for sparse symmetric positive-definite systems. Residual evaluation, correction solves, and iterate updates contribute differently to the energy-error bound, and precision can vary across levels. This supplies an established framework for spending precision where it matters. Better adaptive rules must respect the convergence and rounding assumptions, as well as the costs of storing and applying the sparse operators. <a class="citation" id="cite-59-1" href="#ref-59" aria-label="Source 59">[59]</a></p><p>Jang, Jolivet, and Mary identify a structural failure in mixed-precision GMRES-DR. Its derivation uses simplified relations from Arnoldi iteration and orthogonality whose finite-precision defects can make the method stagnate. Returning to a more general augmented formulation permits lower-precision inner work without those simplifications. The experiments retain precision-sensitive cases and defer hardware-specific timing. The example links a diagnosis to a mathematical repair of the formulation. <a class="citation" id="cite-63-2" href="#ref-63" aria-label="Source 63">[63]</a></p><p>A more focused question is whether the memory saved by compressing a preconditioner outweighs the cost of extra iterations. Khan and Carson report a steam1 case with 21 GMRES iterations against 14 in uniform precision, while storage falls to 74.9 or 42.6 percent of the uniform version under two thresholds. Those figures leave runtime open because reduced traffic competes with extra iterations and other work. The authors leave predicting favorable matrices as further research. Meanwhile doubly adaptive GMRES already combines iteration-dependent and entry-dependent precision automatically, with a reported peak 1.6-fold speedup on nd24k. Figure 6 compares runs that attained the baseline backward error. Table 3 records stagnation on several matrices and shows that one heuristic attained the target on only one matrix. The failures belong in the comparison. The research target is a better, transferable prediction of when these precision choices repay their cost. <a class="citation" id="cite-64-1" href="#ref-64" aria-label="Source 64">[64]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-65-1" href="#ref-65" aria-label="Source 65">[65]</a></p><p>The distinction between a research agent and a solution surrogate also matters economically. Huang and Stuart&#x27;s comparisons of neural operators with classical solvers depend on accuracy and on amortized training and data costs. The plateau they observe for particular Darcy surrogates is not evidence that an agent cannot discover a better sparse solver. For the present programme, the relevant comparison is the final matrix algorithm, its construction costs, and the workload over which those costs are recovered. <a class="citation" id="cite-41-1" href="#ref-41" aria-label="Source 41">[41]</a></p><p>Zhang, Li, and Saad tested a learned multilevel preconditioner on 867 real non-SPD SuiteSparse matrices. Median setup/training time was 135.77 seconds versus 0.18 for the tested AMG. Median solve time also favored AMG, 1.31 seconds versus 8.08. Mean solve time favored the learned method, 9.17 seconds versus 50.87. Its convergence win shares are conditional on valid runs. Separate medians cannot be added to recover paired total times. Cross-matrix transfer and the tails of the cost distribution both matter when choosing the deployment workload. <a class="citation" id="cite-42-1" href="#ref-42" aria-label="Source 42">[42]</a></p><h3>Nonnormal operators and verified sparse solving</h3><p>An inexpensive probe can tempt us to stop while the largest mode remains out of sight. The now-proved Crouzeix conjecture bounds the norm of a matrix function using the maximum absolute value of the corresponding scalar function on the matrix’s numerical range. Take the diagonal matrix from Section 5, with entries 1 and 10⁻⁸. Start a Krylov calculation in the second coordinate. Every power of the matrix keeps it there. The projected range contains only 10⁻⁸. For the identity function, substituting that range into the theorem would give an upper bound of 2 × 10⁻⁸ for a matrix whose norm is 1. The projection shows what the probe reached. A certificate also has to bound what it missed. <a class="citation" id="cite-17-3" href="#ref-17" aria-label="Source 17">[17]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-66-1" href="#ref-66" aria-label="Source 66">[66]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-67-1" href="#ref-67" aria-label="Source 67">[67]</a></p><p>Crouzeix points to an adjacent programme in nonnormal matrix computation. For a function f holomorphic near the numerical range W(A), the reported resolution gives the following bound.</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="||f(A)||_2 &lt;= 2 max_{z in W(A)} |f(z)|.">||<i>f</i>(<i>A</i>)||<sub>2</sub> ≤ 2 max<sub><i>z</i> in <i>W</i>(<i>A</i>)</sub> |<i>f</i>(<i>z</i>)|.</div><p>For normal matrices the spectral theorem already supplies a constant-one bound. The new direction concerns operators for which eigenvalues alone give an incomplete account, including nonsymmetric systems and some directed-graph problems. Crouzeix and Greenbaum use the new result to improve bounds on other spectral regions and apply them to GMRES and rational Krylov methods. Choosing a useful region, approximation, and inner-solve accuracy is a further design problem. A numerical-range bound containing zero can fail to certify GMRES contraction. A valid cutout that excludes zero without surrounding it may help, but requires additional information about the inverse. <a class="citation" id="cite-66-2" href="#ref-66" aria-label="Source 66">[66]</a></p><p>The direction of the approximation matters when estimating a spectral region. For an orthonormal Krylov basis V, W(V* A V) lies inside W(A). This projected set is an inner approximation, while a certificate based on an outer enclosure needs a bound in the other direction. An ordinary largest Ritz value of a Hermitian support matrix is a lower estimate. An outer support line needs an upper bound. Chen and Urschel quantify Krylov approximation of numerical ranges and its limitations in exact arithmetic. A floating-point certificate requires further control. This identifies the information a cheap diagnostic must obtain before its estimate can support a rigorous guarantee. <a class="citation" id="cite-67-2" href="#ref-67" aria-label="Source 67">[67]</a></p><p>Verified sparse solving also has established automatic methods. Rump develops entrywise error bounds for real and complex sparse systems, using inexpensive bounds first and stronger residual-based work when needed. Different factorizations and fill patterns can change verification cost sharply. A new certificate must therefore improve an existing procedure, or apply where its assumptions fail, rather than claim that rigorous checking has not yet been automated. <a class="citation" id="cite-68-1" href="#ref-68" aria-label="Source 68">[68]</a></p><h3>What autonomous solver research already achieves</h3><p>LinearSolveBench released sparse solvers selected from 640 candidates per model on 96 magnetic-diffusion cases. The best passed the specified checks on all 96 and ran about 1.85 times faster than GMRES(50) with BoomerAMG when the original case times were summed. The timed work covered solver creation, setup and solution. Discovery, compilation and independent verification were outside that clock. The release adds a fresh correctness check on the same cases. Speed on a new family is an open question. <a class="citation" id="cite-32-1" href="#ref-32" aria-label="Source 32">[32]</a></p><p>An agent also gets to use the adaptive solvers that already exist. PCGBandit tries configurations from a menu of 33 during a flow simulation and charges for the trials and the fallback. The Shercliff simulation made 7,500 pressure solves. With the reference incomplete-Cholesky preconditioner those solves consumed 59 percent of the runtime. There is a bill worth cutting, and the learner improved the pressure solves. On the electric-potential solves in the same simulation, its costly early choices swallowed much of what it could save later. Someone who claims to have invented a better diagnostic has a working competitor to beat. <a class="citation" id="cite-62-3" href="#ref-62" aria-label="Source 62">[62]</a></p><p>What happens to a human contribution once it can be written down? In a finite-element task in SciCode-Verified, agents kept replacing the specified weak form with its textbook counterpart. Restating the equations at implementation time corrected that choice. Across 64 corrected main problems, expert background raised GPT-5.5 from 65.6 to 90.6 percent, Gemini 3.5 Flash from 51.6 to 87.5 percent, and Claude Opus 4.8 from 57.8 to 78.1 percent. Each model ran once per condition. Every scored subproblem had to pass, and it could pass in either of two software environments. Writing the background and independently rediscovering it were both unpriced. Once every team receives it, a later gain from using it cannot be credited again to a specialist’s presence. <a class="citation" id="cite-35-1" href="#ref-35" aria-label="Source 35">[35]</a></p><p>The autonomous competitor can also question its judge. During AIDE²’s eight-day research run an agent found that one failing test case could crash the scoring of the entire set. It repaired the scoring script. Seven harness changes were accepted. The target suite and outer objective were supplied, but no operator had named that repair. A machine can choose a new diagnostic project inside an existing programme. <a class="citation" id="cite-55-1" href="#ref-55" aria-label="Source 55">[55]</a></p><p>In SVG-EAR, Astra wrote a probe that periodically compared sparse attention with exact attention on a small sample of queries. Its code assigned a budget to each head using the measured error and fell back to dense attention when necessary. Astra also supplied a variance correction. Two additional agents independently rediscovered that correction in exploratory runs. The final code reduced mean runtime by about 18 percent, improved all three mean fidelity measures and passed 43 of 48 hidden checks. None of the twelve evaluated agents passed the whole task. The study assessed the combined changes. The five fidelity failures also occurred in a separate exact-attention implementation. An agent had devised an informative measurement and used it to change the calculation. <a class="citation" id="cite-70-1" href="#ref-70" aria-label="Source 70">[70]</a></p><p>Lu and colleagues hid which equation and coefficients generated a field, supplied sparse measurements and a library of possible equations, and tested the resulting solver on held-out cases. On 278 feasibility-screened cases with unseen templates or shifted coefficients, iterative Codex with GPT-6.1 passed 261. A single-run baseline passed 135. The researchers had specified the field accuracy to achieve. Whether an agent would discover that a different physical quantity needed protecting is the next question. <a class="citation" id="cite-157-1" href="#ref-157" aria-label="Source 157">[157]</a></p><p>Chow and Li developed two adaptive solvers for electronic-structure calculations, one through an autonomous coding system and one by iterating with Codex themselves. On two difficult iron complexes both implementations detected a plateau in the orbital-gradient norm and recovered. On the easy serine peptide both reached the same solution. The human-guided implementation also ran a stability analysis after convergence and took about six times as long as the standard iteration. The autonomous implementation took about as long as the standard one. The development tools differed, and a different calculation may require the extra check. On the nickel complex both adaptive solvers reached internally stable states of slightly higher energy than the standalone orbital trust-region method. Detecting a plateau, verifying internal stability and identifying the physical ground state are three different claims. <a class="citation" id="cite-114-1" href="#ref-114" aria-label="Source 114">[114]</a></p><p>A flow simulation can also finish successfully while supplying the wrong evidence for its conclusion. Somasekharan and colleagues added a visual check to solver logs. A model reading the rendered fields flagged 14 of 16 planted failures. It also flagged all four clean controls. The test harness had omitted plots requested by its own instructions. Give a second agent a different view and it can catch mistakes. Give it an incomplete view and it can veto good work. The skill at issue includes deciding when a missing observation calls for a new calculation and when it calls for opening the rest of the file. Separate notebooks and disciplinary perspectives have helped machine teams find better architectures. The human rival must earn the same credit through what the extra perspective changes. <a class="citation" id="cite-128-2" href="#ref-128" aria-label="Source 128">[128]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-154-1" href="#ref-154" aria-label="Source 154">[154]</a></p><h3 id="optimal-regional-mass-estimate">The best possible regional mass estimate</h3><p>Use the single-transfer model in <a href="#testing-the-proposed-advantage">Section 4</a>. Each donor concentration lies between zero and one, the overlap matrix O is exact, and each reported receiver mass differs from its true value by at most δ. Subtract the known masses of the uniform half-concentration field. Estimate the requested regional mass by taking a weighted sum of these centered readings with coefficients c and adding back the known half-concentration mass of the region. A donor concentration now differs from its center by at most one half. This specialization of optimal recovery gives the following guarantee by the triangle inequality. <a class="citation" id="cite-166-2" href="#ref-166" aria-label="Source 166">[166]</a></p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="absolute mass error &lt;= (1/2) ||w - O^T c||_1 + delta ||c||_1.">absolute mass error ≤ ½ ‖<i>w</i> − <i>O</i><sup>T</sup><i>c</i>‖<sub>1</sub> + δ ‖<i>c</i>‖<sub>1</sub>.</div><p>Here the 1-norm is the sum of absolute entries. The first term prices the part of the regional weights the chosen overlaps fail to represent. The second prices the measurement noise the chosen coefficients can amplify. Both terms are in units of mass. With independent interval bounds on concentrations and noise, choosing their signs to agree with the respective coefficients attains the bound in the worst case for this estimator.</p><p>Minimize that bound over all choices of c. Linear-programming duality identifies its minimum with the largest regional mass change w · z among perturbations z whose entries have magnitude at most one half and whose receiver mass changes Oz have magnitude at most δ in each entry. Take a maximizing z. Add it to the uniform half-concentration field or subtract it. In each case admissible noise can cancel the change at every receiver. The two fields yield identical readings, yet their regional masses differ by twice w · z. Any answer from those readings must miss at least one of them by w · z or more. The minimizing linear estimate attains that worst-case error, even among nonlinear estimates. To price one extra measurement with the same error bound, add its row to O and solve the same problem again. The decrease in the optimum gives the improvement in the best possible worst-case guarantee. It can be zero. A bound for the underlying continuum field still requires information about variation inside the cells. <a class="citation" id="cite-147-9" href="#ref-147" aria-label="Source 147">[147]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-151-13" href="#ref-151" aria-label="Source 151">[151]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-158-3" href="#ref-158" aria-label="Source 158">[158]</a></p><h3 id="incidence-and-numerical-pde">What changes between pure and numerical PDE</h3><p>Point-curve incidences in the work of Raz, Sharir and de Zeeuw, and wave-packet overlaps in Guth, Wang and Zhang, already carry counting arguments back to algebra and theoretical PDE. In numerical PDE the return trip has a particularly exacting test. Carry a missing edge or a lost alignment all the way to a bound on the field at the accuracy somebody needs. The scientist can then use that theorem to judge a particular answer. <a class="citation" id="cite-22-4" href="#ref-22" aria-label="Source 22">[22]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-43-4" href="#ref-43" aria-label="Source 43">[43]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-140-4" href="#ref-140" aria-label="Source 140">[140]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-141-4" href="#ref-141" aria-label="Source 141">[141]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-142-2" href="#ref-142" aria-label="Source 142">[142]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-144-2" href="#ref-144" aria-label="Source 144">[144]</a> There is already a mathematical audience for work that makes this journey. In his Henrici Prize interview, Douglas Arnold described how finite-element theory exposes defects in simulations and points to better methods. Lawrence Guth’s Bôcher Prize citation singled out the use of partitioning and multiscale organization across incidence geometry, harmonic analysis and PDE. The bridge counts as mathematics at both ends. <a class="citation" id="cite-149-1" href="#ref-149" aria-label="Source 149">[149]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-150-1" href="#ref-150" aria-label="Source 150">[150]</a></p></section><section class="essay-notes" id="experimental-protocol"><h2>Appendix C. Experimental protocol</h2><h3>Solver implementations and held-out tests</h3><p>Before the first trial, record the implementation and version, precision, hardware, smoother, coarsening and coarse solve, grid sizes, μ, weight ranges, change generators, budgets and stopping rules. Each preconditioned-CG run requires a fixed linear symmetric positive-definite applied cycle. The three policies respectively keep the old hierarchy intact (F), update its level matrices and smoothers while retaining transfers (U), or rebuild it (R). To certify F, compare the changed weights with the initial weights and use proved α and β for the actual baseline. If only spectral estimates are available, report the policy comparison as empirical. The independent μ-based residual check can still certify answers under all three policies. Acceptance under U supplies no uniform quality bound on the updated hierarchy.</p><p>Begin with manufactured solutions normalized to unit A&#x27;-energy, including directions aligned with the modes missed after a weight change, localized patterns, and generic patterns. Form their right-hand sides consistently and account for construction error. Normalization gives the prescribed absolute energy tolerance a comparable scale across the controlled tests. Then test unknown solutions with a declared right-hand-side normalization and the same prescribed absolute energy tolerance τ. Use four right-hand sides as the primary short workload, with one and a prespecified longer horizon as sensitivity cases. Hold out coefficient-change regimes and larger sizes.</p><p>Keep manufactured reference solutions and exact-error measurements within the independent checker, outside the information supplied to the candidate solver or policy selector. On reference cases, record the first iteration at which the actual energy error and the certified bound each reach the prescribed tolerance. Their difference counts the additional iterations associated with the conservative stopping rule. Measure the corresponding solve time and deployed checking cost separately. Report inflation from validated residual evaluation separately.</p><p>Obtain reference-error measurements in separate calibration runs. Match the timed runs&#x27; inputs, initial states, arithmetic, random seeds, and algorithmic decisions, and compare their residual histories and iteration counts. If the trajectories diverge, attribute the calibration to its own trajectory. Keep the deployed acceptance checker active in timed runs and charge it to solution cost. Account for reference instrumentation and replay under research and validation, identifying shared benchmark costs and costs incurred by each group.</p><p>Time each selector and each fixed policy on paired workloads. Charge transfers, updates, coarse factorizations, setup, solves and deployed independent checks. On failed acceptance, rebuild and retry within a fixed budget, then count unresolved cases. Before evaluation, fix the worthwhile cost-improvement margin, uncertainty rule and allowable failure rate. Report paired total times and outcome distributions at the same accuracy for each workload horizon.</p><p>An elimination project can use AC(2) with a fixed implementation, including its arithmetic, randomization, and input class. Compare any repair with unmodified AC(2), approximate-elimination alternatives such as CAST, and strong multigrid or direct-solver baselines for the declared workload. Reserve graph families as well as seeds, including weakly joined clusters, long narrow connections, graded weights, degree variation, and larger instances. Report fill, peak memory, setup, factor application, convergence, independent error checks, failures, and fallback costs. Separate a single unfamiliar matrix, a short sequence after a structural change, and a long related sequence. A diagnosis should predict which regime repays its cost. <a class="citation" id="cite-26-4" href="#ref-26" aria-label="Source 26">[26]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-48-7" href="#ref-48" aria-label="Source 48">[48]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-62-4" href="#ref-62" aria-label="Source 62">[62]</a></p><p>Broader coarse-space work should include adaptive smoothed aggregation, compatible-relaxation or bootstrap constructions, and hierarchy-reuse policies on a declared SPD family. Compare with online configuration methods and well-chosen fixed settings, not only library defaults. On manageable sizes include an expensive spectral reference. Charge candidate generation, probes, local eigensolves, rejected attempts, rebuilding, and checking. Measure coarse dimension, transfer nonzeros, hierarchy size, memory, and total time. All groups may change support or aggregation and invent alternatives. An exact-coarse-solve theorem must be assessed separately from its recursive implementation. <a class="citation" id="cite-20-5" href="#ref-20" aria-label="Source 20">[20]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-39-2" href="#ref-39" aria-label="Source 39">[39]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-54-3" href="#ref-54" aria-label="Source 54">[54]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-76-1" href="#ref-76" aria-label="Source 76">[76]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-78-4" href="#ref-78" aria-label="Source 78">[78]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-79-3" href="#ref-79" aria-label="Source 79">[79]</a></p><p>A high-probability theorem must state its input assumptions, randomization, and failure-probability dependence. Practical predictions also require the relevant constants. Keep exact small-instance calculations, large-instance estimates, and validated proofs distinct. Independently recompute residuals against the original operator and check the declared kernel and positivity assumptions.</p><p>Across these projects, retain final evaluation cases outside development and record exploratory discoveries separately. Assess a proposed checker correction against the original mathematical requirement. An added application requirement defines a further task. <a class="citation" id="cite-45-1" href="#ref-45" aria-label="Source 45">[45]</a></p><h3 id="sparse-solver-pilot">Sparse-solver pilot</h3><p>Start the solver pilot with a shifted grid Laplacian A(w) = L(w) + μ I, positive edge weights and fixed μ &gt; 0. Build an initial multigrid hierarchy, change the weights once, and solve the changed system A′x = b. Every trial starts with the same initial hierarchy for its operator. A selector has three choices. <a class="citation" id="cite-76-2" href="#ref-76" aria-label="Source 76">[76]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-78-5" href="#ref-78" aria-label="Source 78">[78]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-79-4" href="#ref-79" aria-label="Source 79">[79]</a></p><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Three responses to the same change in edge weights.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Policy</th><th scope="col">Operation after a weight change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>F (fixed)</td><td>Retain the old preconditioner in full, including every level matrix, smoother, transfer and coarse factorization.</td></tr><tr><td>U (updated)</td><td>Retain the transfers. Recompute the Galerkin operators, smoothers and coarse factorization.</td></tr><tr><td>R (rebuilt)</td><td>Build a new hierarchy with the original setup procedure.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Only F keeps the preconditioner covered by the weight-ratio argument in Section 6. Check every policy against the same independent energy-error bound from <a href="#earning-reliability">Section 7</a>. Before designing a selector, find out what choosing well could buy. On development jobs, replay F, U and R from the same saved hierarchy. Repeat paired timings so the apparent saving does not depend on picking a lucky run. Give hindsight all the replay results. Let it pick one action per job to minimize total time while meeting exactly the accuracy and reliability rules the live selector must meet. Charge setup, checking, recovery and failed runs alike. If every job must pass, this reduces to picking the cheapest successful action on each. If no assignment meets the rules, even foreknowledge fails. Compare this free hindsight choice with the fastest eligible baseline, counting both fixed policies and the existing adaptive policy. The gap is a ceiling on what choosing among F, U and R could save on that batch before paying for information.</p><p>The published CAST timings let us put a number on that ceiling for a narrower choice. CAST reports 28, 24, 27, 26 and 23 systems on its five Chimera graphs. At 250 right-hand sides, weighting the published graph means by those counts gives about 2,313 seconds for CAST-1 throughout and 2,251 if hindsight chooses one of the two CAST variants per graph. The entire saving comes from the third graph. Its 27 systems save about 2.3 seconds apiece. The displayed numbers are rounded means of per-system medians over five factor draws. A policy choosing separately for every matrix could have a different ceiling. On the bench we can price this particular choice at about 62 seconds per batch before paying for a probe. On a deployment we need its matrices, right-hand sides, reuse horizon and best existing policy. <a class="citation" id="cite-48-8" href="#ref-48" aria-label="Source 48">[48]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-120-1" href="#ref-120" aria-label="Source 120">[120]</a></p><p>The same distinction shows up on an eight-vertex cycle small enough for exact arithmetic. Across four changes of weights, four right-hand sides apiece, F, U and R took 65, 48 and 46 conjugate-gradient steps. Picking the fewest steps on each job also took 46. A selector earned nothing on that score. Setup could still make updating cheaper than rebuilding. Rasouli and collaborators actually timed related updates after mesh deformation. On their stretched mesh a fine-level update beat rebuilding through the ninth case, and rebuilding paid on the tenth. Their fine-level policy updated the operator. Our fixed policy retains the old preconditioner in full. Replaying these choices is established practice. Once these comparisons are public, the agent can run them too. Recognizing a documented crossover on the original workload supplies no evidence of a continuing centaur advantage. <a class="citation" id="cite-76-3" href="#ref-76" aria-label="Source 76">[76]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-120-2" href="#ref-120" aria-label="Source 120">[120]</a></p><p>On the eight-cycle, consecutive edge weights alternate between 1 and 1/64 and the diagonal shift is 1/16. Rescale all weights by 5/4, exchange the two parities, raise the original strong weights to 4, or set every weight to 1. The rebuilt hierarchy pairs adjacent vertices along the heavier parity, retaining the original pairing on a tie. Its two-level preconditioner uses one damped Jacobi sweep on each side of an exact Galerkin correction. Fine PCG starts at zero and checks the original residual against an absolute energy-error tolerance of 10⁻⁴. On the exchange alone, F, U and R took 18, 13 and 11 total steps across the four right-hand sides. The supplied code specifies the vectors, reruns all policies with rational arithmetic and separately reconstructs the graph-level CAST comparison from Table 5. <a class="citation" id="cite-48-9" href="#ref-48" aria-label="Source 48">[48]</a> <span id="cite-120-5" aria-hidden="true"></span><a class="citation" id="cite-120-7" href="#ref-120" aria-label="Source 120">[120]</a></p><h3 id="arithmetic-comparison">Arithmetic comparison</h3><p>The arithmetic pilot shows what a finite screen can rule out before anyone tries to reconstruct equations for a cover. Can an irreducible degree-23 polynomial over the rationals have all twenty-three roots real and Galois group M₂₃? Huang and collaborators solved the problem without the condition on the roots. Even the branch data for their construction took work to choose. Seven complex covers had the chosen branching. Galois conjugation permuted six and fixed one. They found equations over the rationals for that one. Forty-digit expansions and a change of scale recovered its equations. For the other covers even sixty digits left some coefficients unrecognizable. The team combined data from the six other permutation triples and changed scale again. The authors describe AI-assisted computation under human direction. They independently checked the final claims in Magma and PARI/GP without AI. <a class="citation" id="cite-110-1" href="#ref-110" aria-label="Source 110">[110]</a></p><p>Try the published polynomial on the new demand. An exact Sturm count gives seven real roots. The other sixteen form eight conjugate pairs. Choosing another rational parameter cannot fix it. The real parameter line, with infinity included, stays connected after its only real branch point is removed. Complex conjugation keeps the same action away from that point, and the authors checked the exceptional point separately. A stronger result was already on the shelf. Fried and Dèbes exclude a full-group totally real specialization of any regular three-branch-point M₂₃ Galois cover over the rationals. In the relevant three-point case the group would have to be generated by two involutions and would be dihedral. M₂₃ is not. Malle’s constructions for M₁₁ and M₁₂ show why trying a different family is serious mathematics. Give every team these results before the clock starts. Reading a known exclusion can save work. Rediscovering it earns no extra credit. <a class="citation" id="cite-110-2" href="#ref-110" aria-label="Source 110">[110]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-116-1" href="#ref-116" aria-label="Source 116">[116]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-118-1" href="#ref-118" aria-label="Source 118">[118]</a></p><p>Here is a fresh structural pilot. Try four ordered rational branch points, an interval where all the sheets are real, and a genus-zero source curve so its equations might be manageable. Cap the four branching orders at four. Before reconstructing anything, enumerate the permutations that could describe such a cover. In the natural action of M₂₃ on 23 letters the resulting test reduces to four permutations (a, ab, bc, c), where a, b and c are involutions and ab and bc have order four. Their product is automatically the identity. They must also generate the full group. Counting up to simultaneous conjugation in M₂₃ gives 480 candidate classes. Exactly 16 generate M₂₃. An independent orbit count gives the same answer. Anyone planning to recover equations for a rejected class can stop immediately. <a class="citation" id="cite-118-2" href="#ref-118" aria-label="Source 118">[118]</a> <span id="cite-120-3" aria-hidden="true"></span><a class="citation" id="cite-120-8" href="#ref-120" aria-label="Source 120">[120]</a></p><p>The sixteen survivors do not settle the arithmetic. Each describes a complex cover whose degree-23 source curve has genus zero. The sixteen generating classes in the restricted screen lie in a full braid orbit of 2,456 generating classes. The curve that parametrizes this orbit is a different object. Häfner computed genus 447 for its ordered parameter curve. The finite screen has identified where a rational point would have to be sought in the appropriate real region. It has produced no such point. The next arithmetic question is whether the relevant part of that curve admits a rational point. Finding an obstruction there could retire the route. Passing the finite group test alone gives none of the sixteen candidates equations over the rationals. <a class="citation" id="cite-119-1" href="#ref-119" aria-label="Source 119">[119]</a> <span id="cite-120-4" aria-hidden="true"></span><a class="citation" id="cite-120-9" href="#ref-120" aria-label="Source 120">[120]</a></p><p>For the M₂₃ screen the branch index is 23 minus the number of cycles, fixed letters included. Genus zero requires the four indices to sum to 44. The two end involutions contribute 8 apiece. Under the order cap the two middle permutations must each have order four and index 14. Fix the middle involution b. The 1,008 possible endpoints split into three orbits under its centralizer, which has order 2,688. For each left representative take the right orbits under its stabilizer. This gives 480 ordered classes. Burnside’s lemma independently counts the same orbits. There are 43,008 transitive labeled pairs for fixed b. Every such triple generates M₂₃ and has trivial stabilizer, yielding 43,008/2,688 = 16. The script prints a witness, verifies the branch product, and checks rationality of its conjugacy classes. A rational class condition alone cannot supply equations over the rationals. <a class="citation" id="cite-110-3" href="#ref-110" aria-label="Source 110">[110]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-118-3" href="#ref-118" aria-label="Source 118">[118]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-119-2" href="#ref-119" aria-label="Source 119">[119]</a> <span id="cite-120-6" aria-hidden="true"></span><a class="citation" id="cite-120-10" href="#ref-120" aria-label="Source 120">[120]</a></p><h3>Comparing teams on the same brief</h3><div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>What would establish each claim?</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Claim</th><th scope="col">Required evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Better performance on a stated workload</td><td>Independent measurements at the same accuracy, including setup, checking, and recovery.</td></tr><tr><td>Transfer beyond development cases</td><td>Withheld families with meaningful changes in geometry, coefficients, topology, or other relevant structure.</td></tr><tr><td>A uniform mathematical guarantee</td><td>A valid argument covering the stated class, with its assumptions and parameter dependence explicit.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Decide in advance when an obstruction answers the question. Suppose a team proves that a specified family of cheap observations gives identical answers on two allowed changes that require different actions to meet the cost and accuracy target. That can settle whether the proposed policy class will suffice. In a cover project, a proof excluding the prescribed class of realizations earns the corresponding credit if its scope settles the declared question. A stalled search earns neither verdict. Flag in advance which briefs admit a positive construction, a decisive obstruction, or both. Report the two kinds of result separately as well as success under each brief. In the four-solve performance pilot the positive outcome still requires a faster accepted method. Evidence for stopping a search and any demonstrable work it saves get their own record.</p><p>Hold the lead agent fixed. Give one team a specialist. Give the other a second agent chosen on separate development problems, free to use a different model, tools or proof strategy. Each gets the same starting evidence, access to the same tools and the same total budget. For a brief that leaves the target open, begin charging both teams before anyone chooses the problem. Also run the lead on its own with the resources available for extra attempts. Let <i>C</i><sub>H</sub> and <i>C</i><sub>A</sub> be the total research costs at which the human-agent pair and the machine pair respectively first produce an independently accepted result. Count advice, checking and failed searches. Assign infinite cost to a run that exhausts its protocol without an accepted result. At budget b the human partner’s advantage is</p><div class="math-display" role="math" tabindex="0" aria-label="Delta(b) = Pr(C_H &lt;= b) - Pr(C_A &lt;= b).">Δ(<i>b</i>) = Pr(<i>C</i><sub>H</sub> ≤ <i>b</i>) - Pr(<i>C</i><sub>A</sub> ≤ <i>b</i>).</div><p>At three hours Archon’s run with the human blueprint had finished and the other run had not. At four hours both had finished. Repeat the partner swap across fresh problems. Report correct rescues and harmful vetoes as well as the net gain. A generalist paired with the lead tests whether the gain requires subject expertise. A specialist working with the same software and sources without generative AI tests whether the machine helps the person too. Let the strongest machine-only procedure found in development compete for the same budget, even if it uses parallel independent searches instead of a pair in conversation. <a class="citation" id="cite-90-3" href="#ref-90" aria-label="Source 90">[90]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-103-1" href="#ref-103" aria-label="Source 103">[103]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-104-1" href="#ref-104" aria-label="Source 104">[104]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-123-2" href="#ref-123" aria-label="Source 123">[123]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-124-4" href="#ref-124" aria-label="Source 124">[124]</a></p><p>In the open-ended transfer experiment, the unit of comparison is a portfolio of follow-up problems selected from the same precursor and historical record. Predeclare time and research costs, eligibility for a new claim, the process for checking originality, and how independent assessors will judge mathematical substance. Keep the proposed question and success criterion on record before its result is known. Report questions proposed, questions abandoned, verified results and assessor disagreement. Separate value attributable to selecting the problem from value attributable to solving a named one. Include previously unanticipated follow-ups that meet the criteria. <a class="citation" id="cite-4-7" href="#ref-4" aria-label="Source 4">[4]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-132-3" href="#ref-132" aria-label="Source 132">[132]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-133-4" href="#ref-133" aria-label="Source 133">[133]</a></p><p>A human can help a model because their errors overlap less, and lose that benefit by deferring to it at the wrong time. Steyvers and colleagues combined independent image judgments and confidence scores under the same fitted rule. Human-machine pairs gained most over pairs of different networks when the partners had comparable accuracy and the images were noisy. Hemmer and colleagues instead held human participation fixed. On fifteen images, changing which classifier advised participants lowered mean team error from 24.7 to 14.6 percent although both classifiers missed four images alone. AutoAnalysis reports that in its subset comparing collaborations with both solo conditions, average team performance fell below the stronger individual condition. None of these designs asks who will invent a missing mathematical test. <a class="citation" id="cite-103-2" href="#ref-103" aria-label="Source 103">[103]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-104-2" href="#ref-104" aria-label="Source 104">[104]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-129-1" href="#ref-129" aria-label="Source 129">[129]</a></p><p>For each portfolio, define the project sampling frame, intended population, acceptance rules and distribution of briefs before execution. Classify projects by the deliverable specified at entry. Fix the treatment of genuinely mixed briefs and pair analysis by project. A construction or obstruction counts toward the primary full-project outcome only when it meets the scope announced for that brief. Record separately which result was obtained, which claimed stopping decisions independent assessors accepted, and which downstream costs the evidence supports. Report construction-only and obstruction-eligible briefs separately so different mixes of outcomes remain visible. Within the method pilot, method acceptance requires the predetermined accuracy, reliability and deployment-cost improvement on its withheld workload.</p><p>Use several participants and repeated seeds, with independent final assessment. Assess competence before assignment. Fix eligibility, allocation, model and harness versions, independent project count and the primary budget-accounting rule in advance. Pilot data can inform a precision or power analysis while the final evaluation stays reserved. On development briefs choose a machine adviser with strong performance alongside the lead. Allow different model families, private contexts, rival representations and computational tools. Freeze the pairing and the best unrestricted machine-only procedure before the fresh briefs arrive. Give every arm the same underlying evidence, access to tools and freedom to abandon a formulation. On matrix projects this includes the operator, grounding convention and tolerance. On arithmetic projects specify the target group, base field and required local or real behavior. Choosing a cover, branch data, or a different route belongs to the research team. Screen fresh briefs for known public answers before assignment. Assess any independently justified solution meeting the stated target, including a construction the assessors did not anticipate. A proof excluding one candidate family earns credit for that narrower result. Additional seeds measure variation within a project. Inference across projects draws its replication from independent projects.</p><p>Record person-hours, elapsed time, model expenditure, computation, checking, failed attempts and deployment costs separately. Report equal-time and equal-cost comparisons. Before combining costs, specify a human-hour price and the number of deployments over which discovery is amortized. Report sensitivity to declared alternative prices and horizons. A stop at one budget leaves success at larger budgets unknown. Fix any additional budgets in advance. Use the same economic rules in the specialist-alone arm when the stronger collaboration claim is tested. When a team uses a probe to choose its next step, record the decision it could change and the cost of obtaining the answer. Raiffa and Schlaifer give the formal comparison when the actions, beliefs and payoffs are specified. <a class="citation" id="cite-122-1" href="#ref-122" aria-label="Source 122">[122]</a></p><h3>Evidence about choosing a partner</h3><p>Christian Coester wanted to know how much farther an online algorithm must travel than a competitor who knows the coming requests. Each request is a set the algorithm has to reach. An earlier lower bound in the plane used three stages of line requests and six constants. Coester wanted a continuously moving line. GPT-5 initially reproduced the old bound. Coester says a fleeting suggestion of π/2 in its reasoning may have helped him see the semicircle traced as the line rotates. Following the semicircle would cost the online algorithm π/2 while the optimal route cost one. To force the bound even on an algorithm that left the arc, his construction needed to switch pivots. That introduced a defect. It could switch pivots infinitely often at one instant and never move on. <a class="citation" id="cite-130-3" href="#ref-130" aria-label="Source 130">[130]</a></p><p>GPT-5 supplied the repair. Require a small positive separation before switching pivots, then let that separation tend to zero. For the higher-dimensional problem Coester was trying to make the construction work in three dimensions. The model suggested applying the planar argument separately in orthogonal two-dimensional planes. Coester corrected errors in the execution. His geometry gave the machine a viable problem to solve. Its decomposition gave him a proof route his focus on three dimensions had obscured. We know what each investigator brought to the proof. Nobody ran the comparison in which another agent got Coester’s seat. <a class="citation" id="cite-130-4" href="#ref-130" aria-label="Source 130">[130]</a></p><p>A short comparison shows what directing a search can buy. AlphaEvolve was asked to build a long ±1 sequence whose sums along every progression d, 2d, 3d, … stay between −2 and 2. It reached length 200. With a hint to try approximately multiplicative sequences it found length 380 in the same amount of search time. The known optimum is 1,160. The hint changed where the machine looked on a problem with a known answer. Its authors did not compare the time to devise that hint with the time a machine adviser would have needed. <a class="citation" id="cite-131-1" href="#ref-131" aria-label="Source 131">[131]</a></p><p>The reason to bring a specialist is strongest when their independent experience changes a choice with consequences. The Grothendieck operators knew what fourteen sessions of restricted lemmas had failed to settle. Bolzano gives the reverse handoff. Its agents invented a nested search in which each collision determines the next place to look. Pavel Hubáček found the obstruction showing why revealing a collision could still leave a reduction empty-handed. An equally persistent machine rival deserves its own failures, notebooks and time to change its mind. Comparing the partners on the theorem each eventually produces is harder than comparing their advice. It is also closer to what a mathematician wants to know. <a class="citation" id="cite-105-7" href="#ref-105" aria-label="Source 105">[105]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-125-2" href="#ref-125" aria-label="Source 125">[125]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-128-3" href="#ref-128" aria-label="Source 128">[128]</a></p><p>There is already a comparison of two machines starting from the same paper. Both tried extending a result about losing positions in Wythoff Nim, a game played with two piles of tokens. The predicted integer shift failed in the larger family. One agent retreated to the original game. The other followed two citations to a theorem with a fractional shift and proposed a conjecture covering both cases. Across a blinded review of twelve pairs, an author of the seed papers preferred eight conjectures from the method that tracked changes between theorems and four from the ordinary search. All were proposals. Two machine investigators had found different mathematics to pursue. <a class="citation" id="cite-135-3" href="#ref-135" aria-label="Source 135">[135]</a></p><p>Does adding a person improve that choice? In a blinded language-research study, one researcher’s reranking replaced 32 of the machine’s 49 choices. Both lists scored above human-written proposals on novelty. Difference of taste is easy to establish. Its payoff takes longer. In a follow-up, 43 researchers executed assigned projects. The machine-origin ideas lost significantly more of their initial ratings on all four shared measures. Across the 43 projects the final differences between origins were statistically inconclusive. A plausible role for a specialist is to recognize which attractive proposal will survive contact with the work. <a class="citation" id="cite-136-1" href="#ref-136" aria-label="Source 136">[136]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-137-2" href="#ref-137" aria-label="Source 137">[137]</a></p><p>A weaker investigator can be the better partner. Minghao Liu and colleagues took an image classifier and changed its partner. The human answers were 81.9 percent accurate, the second classifier’s 90.0 percent. Yet at least one partner had the right answer on 95.8 percent of images in the mixed pair, against 92.3 percent for the two machines. Those ceilings assume someone always knows whom to trust. Handing the base model’s low-confidence cases to its partner also favored the human on these data. The human’s errors fell in different places. <a class="citation" id="cite-126-1" href="#ref-126" aria-label="Source 126">[126]</a></p><p>Different habits can also make partners worse. Jones and colleagues asked pairs to describe and identify the same abstract figures over five rounds. Two copies of GPT-5 reached 99 percent accuracy by round four. A human paired with GPT-5 reached 70 percent in round five. Shared habits helped two machines understand each other. On the images, the weaker partner covered errors the stronger one missed. In the naming game, the mixed pair struggled to make use of what each saw. A mathematician needs both abilities. Spot the overlooked distinction and express it in a form the other investigator can use. <a class="citation" id="cite-126-2" href="#ref-126" aria-label="Source 126">[126]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-127-1" href="#ref-127" aria-label="Source 127">[127]</a></p><p>In one SciPiVot case a 32-fold sweep in spring stiffness moved a simulated support’s deflection only from about 212 to 222 millimeters. All six runs hit the travel limit. The agent proposed changing the number of springs. The senior researcher asked how many springs carried the load and whether the limit had masked the response to stiffness. The two plans called for different experiments. Across 248 decisions from 26 projects, PhD students and postdocs new to the projects, with AI available, selected the senior researcher’s recorded correction in 73.4 percent of cases. The best tested model with the project record scored 63.5 percent. Both plans were supplied. Agreement with a retrospective judgment tells us who recognized the plan. On a live project somebody has to invent the second plan, run the test and decide what its answer means. <a class="citation" id="cite-61-2" href="#ref-61" aria-label="Source 61">[61]</a></p><p>What happens when someone has to choose the next experiment live? Savage and del Rio Chanona had six chemical-engineering postgraduates guide a Bayesian optimizer through a coiled-tube reactor design problem. They could choose starting designs and then pick among proposals over 32 iterations. The human-guided search found better designs early than the standard optimizer in 32 unassisted runs. The lead narrowed as evaluations accumulated. The test used a surrogate fitted to 75 flow simulations. The guided procedure had the early lead. It also started from different designs and offered a choice of proposals. On separate synthetic tests, choosing randomly from such a menu sometimes matched or beat the standard optimizer. Give the same menu and budget to an agent and a generalist on the reactor before attributing its lead to engineering judgment. <a class="citation" id="cite-113-1" href="#ref-113" aria-label="Source 113">[113]</a></p><p>Kasparov’s Advanced Chess gives the human-computer partnership a name and a challenge. Two amateurs won a 2005 freestyle tournament. They describe selecting promising moves, splitting the lines and cross-checking them with different engines. Their competitors had different equipment and preparation. Give a rival the procedure and the engines. What can the player still see first? <a class="citation" id="cite-123-3" href="#ref-123" aria-label="Source 123">[123]</a></p><p>Calling the human contribution “taste” explains too little. Yisong Yue locates mathematical taste in human experience while giving the Caltech-AIM machine a role in proposing conjectures, connections and strategies. Experience matters here if it changes which explanation gets a hearing. A specialist who has watched a fit hide a missing physical term has a reason to inspect the integral even when the residual looks excellent. An agent trained on different failures could acquire the same suspicion. Which partner follows it to a decisive check? <a class="citation" id="cite-91-1" href="#ref-91" aria-label="Source 91">[91]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-101-4" href="#ref-101" aria-label="Source 101">[101]</a> Ding counted the attention to successor questions in 58 human papers and public AI accounts of eleven problems. The human texts gave follow-ups more space. A comparison of what authors chose to publish is evidence about exposition. After42 gave agents the opportunity to nominate questions and found that its shortlist reproduced expert consensus about as well as individual experts in one subfield. Neither study followed the competing selections to their mathematical consequences. <a class="citation" id="cite-134-4" href="#ref-134" aria-label="Source 134">[134]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-143-1" href="#ref-143" aria-label="Source 143">[143]</a></p><p>A second investigator can be a machine. On one problem in the second First Proof challenge, a critic that had followed the revisions accepted seven drafts of the same argument. A fresh critic rejected all seven. The referees found the submitted proof incomplete. On another problem the critic rejected every draft of a proof the referees accepted up to minor repairs. A critic can save a team from a bad proof. It can also cost the team a good one. <a class="citation" id="cite-124-5" href="#ref-124" aria-label="Source 124">[124]</a></p><p>In the same batch the human referees found a gap in a proof about valuated matroids. A proposed formula was supposed to produce a flag of valuated matroids. One citation established a special case. The other did not address the claim. The referees thought the formula likely to work, but the proof still owed its central step. A machine critic had accepted it. A fresh model reading the draft spotted the weak point and called it minor. After seeing the human solution it recognized how much work the proof still needed. The referees earned credit for judging the size of the gap from the submitted argument and its citations alone. <a class="citation" id="cite-124-6" href="#ref-124" aria-label="Source 124">[124]</a></p><p>Aletheia exposed a different limit of proof checking. An earlier version tackled 700 entries then listed as open in the Erdős problem database. It returned 212 possible solutions. The reviewing team judged 63 technically correct, 13 responsive to the intended question and four of those new full or partial solutions. Fifty technically correct answers missed their target. A critic could have compared the proposed statement with the original question before anybody spent time proving it. Wang and collaborators found a related failure in a different task. Across seven models, 60.6 percent of rewrites into a more abstract vocabulary widened the asserted domain. A faithful translation carries the restriction. A genuine generalization needs a proof. Who catches the difference before the team proves the wrong theorem? <a class="citation" id="cite-3-4" href="#ref-3" aria-label="Source 3">[3]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-10-1" href="#ref-10" aria-label="Source 10">[10]</a></p><p>Once a faithful statement and a candidate are in hand, a proof assistant can expose a particular unsolved obligation and a library can make trying lemmas cheap. Measure that advantage from the point where it actually begins. AlphaProof Nexus proved a real-valued version of a problem on Ben Green’s list. Green clarified that the functions in question were complex-valued. A numerical search supplied a candidate counterexample on the cyclic group of order three. The team formalized the new question and the agent verified the counterexample. A proof checker gave a decisive answer twice. Green knew which question he had meant. Give his clarification to every team. The test for a structural specialist is whether they can spot a new mismatch from evidence the agent also has. <a class="citation" id="cite-115-1" href="#ref-115" aria-label="Source 115">[115]</a></p><h3>Testing the proposed diagnosis</h3><p>Keep natural collaboration intact in the primary comparison. In the checkpoint study, elicit the lead’s, specialist’s and machine adviser’s diagnoses independently before revealing anyone else’s. Record which failures both machines miss, which a human also misses and which objections turn out to be false. In a randomized analyst study, fifty professionals followed the assigned agent preference on 86 percent of four choices among defensible alternatives. A person who sees the agent’s answer first may never put their independent judgment on the record. <a class="citation" id="cite-74-1" href="#ref-74" aria-label="Source 74">[74]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-124-7" href="#ref-124" aria-label="Source 124">[124]</a></p><p>Prespecify checkpoint selection and keep unsuccessful interventions. Freeze the evidence, method, agent context and learned state. Each adviser states the predicted failure, test and scope of repair before seeing the reserved outcomes. Conceal authorship and randomize repeated copies of the same lead to a human proposal, a machine proposal or no proposal. Use the same evaluator and remaining compute allowance. Charge for producing the advice. Record whether a correct objection was acted on and whether the repair worked. This distinguishes finding the failure from persuading a partner or implementing the fix. Full-project costs include reaching the checkpoint.</p><p>Classify cases before outcomes by the representation change, information in cheap diagnostics and cost of further experiments. The diagnostic hypothesis predicts a larger advantage over the machine adviser where the available signals fit several costly explanations and the agents tend to favor the same wrong one. Randomize access to the same additional diagnostic information across arms with a prespecified information control. Score intermediate predictions independently. A smaller specialist premium after disclosure is consistent with the mechanism. A success ceiling or information that compensates for implementation or orchestration can produce the same pattern. Prespecify checks for those alternatives in both portfolios.</p><p>Calibrate autonomous difficulty on separate development projects to assess floor and ceiling effects. Estimate the natural portfolio contrast first. In a separate analysis, match or stratify on novelty, available theory, ambiguity, and the cost and informativeness of feedback. Matching on ambiguity removes differences in its frequency. Unmeasured differences can still matter. After each round freeze the shared archive before releasing fresh cases, and keep their reserved results sealed until assessment is complete. Report the cost and downstream benefit of retrieval separately from the incremental benefit of live specialist help.</p><h3>Decision rules</h3><p>Declare δ as the minimum worthwhile increase in accepted discovery probability, distinct from the improvement required to accept a method. For both the human-partner-minus-machine-partner and specialist-minus-generalist contrasts, a lower confidence bound above δ supports a worthwhile gain. An upper bound below δ sets aside a gain of that size for the tested scope. An interval containing δ leaves it unresolved. If the specialist beats the machine partner but the generalist comparison excludes a worthwhile additional gain, report the benefit of assistance and the upper bound on specialization. <a class="citation" id="cite-92-1" href="#ref-92" aria-label="Source 92">[92]</a></p><p>To compare fields, let D(b) be the human partner’s gain over the machine partner in sparse computation minus the corresponding gain in structural mathematics at budget b. Declare a comparative margin η, zero for a directional claim or positive for a minimum worthwhile difference. A lower confidence bound above η supports that cross-portfolio contrast. An upper bound below η sets aside the declared difference for the tested scope. An interval containing η leaves the ranking unresolved. A claim that specialization itself contributes more in sparse computation also needs the cross-portfolio difference of the specialist-minus-generalist gains, with its own prespecified margin. Significance in one portfolio and an inconclusive result in the other give no such test.</p><p>Fix the sample size and stopping rule or use a prespecified sequential procedure with valid coverage. The joint confidence procedure must account for all planned contrasts, project pairing, repeated participants, repeated runs within projects and repeated inspection. Compare against the strongest frozen machine-only procedure before claiming a centaur advantage. Include both solo arms for a claim about beating both individual partners. New agent versions warrant fresh cases under stated application scopes and acceptance rules. Retain the results for the versions that produced them.</p><h3 id="comparing-investigators-on-the-mesh-and-sumset-problems">Comparing investigators on the mesh and sumset problems</h3><p>Have outside collaborators supply fresh mesh and sumset problems before either team proposes a solution. Include other authors’ ruler families and meshes from another group. Give both teams equal access to whoever needs the result. On each brief run a specialist with one agent and two agents free to take different approaches. At the same error tolerance, did the specialist’s design need less storage on unseen meshes? On unseen addition tables, did it prove a tighter bound or cover a larger family at the same cost? Compare partners within each brief before comparing fields. Include generalists and solo specialists, and count failures and false vetoes. <a class="citation" id="cite-89-2" href="#ref-89" aria-label="Source 89">[89]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-90-4" href="#ref-90" aria-label="Source 90">[90]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-105-8" href="#ref-105" aria-label="Source 105">[105]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-124-8" href="#ref-124" aria-label="Source 124">[124]</a></p><p>On one set of fresh mesh cases, let each team question the receiving scientist. On matched cases, give both teams identical written requirements covering the admissible regions, measurement precision and storage budget. Score the guarantee each team produces and charge the interviews, design and verification to its total cost. In the interview cases, record when each team first states a requirement that affects the guarantee and when it produces a measurement that addresses that requirement. An advantage that persists under identical requirements would give stronger evidence for mathematical design.</p><p>Start with a feasibility pilot on the channel remap described in <a href="#testing-the-proposed-advantage">Section 4</a>. Have a receiving scientist supply the patch family and tolerance before the teams see the reserved meshes. Fix the allowed mass summaries and coding rules in both arms. Give both teams the same access to the checker and disclose whether it supplies cheap scores during development or judges only the final guarantee. At each prespecified checkpoint, record which team found a consequential failure, whether its diagnosis became an executable test, and the time spent handing it over and checking it. Test on reserved meshes and keep the project-level paired outcomes and costs for planning a larger study. A finite pilot measures feasibility and performance on those cases. A proof is what covers the stated mesh family. The broader ranking needs independent projects and a sample size chosen for that comparison.</p><p>Have analysts and additive combinatorialists each work on problems in both fields, with the order varied and a fresh case each time. Keep cases from an investigator’s own research line identifiable in the analysis. Let independent judges assess the results without team names. An advantage that follows people into their home territory points toward accumulated experience. A gain that recurs across specialists on problems with an unstable inverse points toward that operation. Either would tell us more than a comparison between my favorite theorem and somebody else’s solver. This experiment has yet to be run.</p><p>Hand both teams an unfamiliar discovery. Leave its successor unnamed. Have each propose a question, explain why either answer would matter and commit to what would count as progress. Then exchange anonymized proposals and give independent investigators equal resources to pursue them. Score the question before execution and the result afterward. Keep an arm in which each team pursues its own idea, so that the insight needed to carry it out can count too. A promising false conjecture that uncovers a sharp obstruction can beat a true one that changes nothing. Circling the sum-product conjecture in advance would give away the decision to look there. <a class="citation" id="cite-132-4" href="#ref-132" aria-label="Source 132">[132]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-133-5" href="#ref-133" aria-label="Source 133">[133]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-134-5" href="#ref-134" aria-label="Source 134">[134]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-135-4" href="#ref-135" aria-label="Source 135">[135]</a> <a class="citation" id="cite-137-3" href="#ref-137" aria-label="Source 137">[137]</a></p></section>
